BEGIN;

-- trade-payables-accounts-payable-and-supplier-finance
UPDATE public.essays
   SET content = $c$<p><strong>In brief</strong></p><ul><li><p>A trade payable is the simplest liability on the balance sheet, for goods or services received and invoiced, with amount and date known. That certainty separates it from an accrual (received, not yet invoiced) and a provision (timing or amount uncertain).</p></li><li><p>Payables are recognised when control of the goods passes or the service is performed, measured at the invoice amount because discounting a short-term balance changes nothing material, and kept in current liabilities because they belong to the operating cycle.</p></li><li><p>A payable leaves the balance sheet only when the obligation is paid, cancelled or expires, or when its terms change substantially. Supplier finance arrangements test that rule. Since 2024 PSAK 207 and PSAK 107 require entities to disclose them so that users can see how much of "trade payables" a bank has already paid.</p></li></ul><hr><p>PT Pertamina Hulu Energi and subsidiaries (PHE) report trade payables of 1,091,822 at 31 December 2024 against 883,688 a year earlier, in USD thousand (PHE FS 2024, statement of financial position, Daftar 1/2, PDF p. 12; "Daftar" is the printed page). The line is 7.1 percent of total liabilities and 17.7 percent of current liabilities (5.3 and 14.7 percent a year earlier), and 3.6 percent of total assets (2.9 percent). Beside it PHE reports other payables of 613,718 (481,963) and other current liabilities of 224 (324), which this page also covers.</p><p>After reading it a student should be able to say when a payable exists, record it through its life in journals, judge whether a supplier finance arrangement has turned it into a borrowing, and read a payables note.</p><p>All amounts are in USD thousand unless stated. Figures from PHE's statements are cited to their note. Every other number is labelled an illustration.</p><h2>1. What a liability is, and which liabilities are current</h2><p>A liability is a present obligation of the entity to transfer an economic resource as a result of past events (<em>Conceptual Framework for Financial Reporting</em> 2018, para. 4.26). For a payable the past event is the delivery of goods or the performance of a service. An undelivered purchase order for drilling chemicals is no liability yet.</p><p>The lecture notes define a current liability as one settled within a year with a resulting cash outflow. The 2018 framework drops the probability language from the definition, so uncertainty about the outflow affects recognition and measurement, and PSAK 201 adds the operating cycle to the one-year test.</p><p><strong>Current or non-current.</strong> PSAK 201 <em>Presentation of Financial Statements</em> (formerly PSAK 1, IAS 1) classifies a liability as current if any of four tests holds: the entity expects to settle it in its normal operating cycle, holds it for trading, must settle it within twelve months after the reporting date, or has no right at that date to defer settlement for at least twelve months (para. 69). Trade payables and accruals for operating costs are part of the working capital used in the operating cycle, so they are current even when due more than twelve months later (para. 70). PHE's policy uses the twelve-month and operating cycle tests (Note 3t, Daftar 5/70, PDF p. 87). The 2024 amendments to PSAK 201 on covenants and the right to defer settlement bite mainly on loans; see the long-term bank loans essay.</p><p><strong>Payable, accrual or provision.</strong> PSAK 237 (formerly PSAK 57, IAS 37) draws the line (para. 11). <em>Trade payables</em> are liabilities for goods or services received or supplied that have been invoiced or formally agreed with the supplier. <em>Accruals</em> are liabilities for goods or services received or supplied that have not been invoiced or formally agreed. <em>Provisions</em> are liabilities of uncertain timing or amount. For example (illustration), three items at a PHE subsidiary on 31 December 2024:</p><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Item</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>What is known</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Line</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Workover rig hire for November, invoiced 20 December, 1,800 due on 19 January</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>amount and date</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>trade payables</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Helicopter flying hours in December, logged at 240 but not yet invoiced</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>amount nearly certain, no invoice</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>accrued expenses</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>A contractor's claim of 3,000 for standby days, disputed</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>amount and timing uncertain</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>provision or contingent liability</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>The accrued expenses essay and the decommissioning provision essay own the second and third rows.</p><h2>2. Recognising and measuring accounts payable</h2><p>A payable arises from a time lag between receiving goods or services and paying for them. The lecture notes tie recognition to the transfer of control. For goods that depends on the shipping terms. Under FOB (free on board) shipping point the payable arises when the seller hands the goods to the carrier, under FOB destination when they arrive (see the inventories essay). For a service the trigger is performance.</p><p><strong>Initial measurement.</strong> A trade payable is a financial liability under PSAK 109 <em>Financial Instruments</em> (formerly PSAK 71, IFRS 9) and is first measured at fair value. For a short-term payable with no stated interest, fair value is the invoice amount, because discounting 30 or 60 days of credit changes nothing material. PHE states its payables at their "notional amounts, which approximate their fair values" (Note 3, financial liabilities, Daftar 5/46, PDF p. 63) and reports that its trade payables are unsecured and non-interest bearing (Note 19, Daftar 5/113, PDF p. 130). Afterwards they are carried at amortised cost, which for a non-interest-bearing payable is the amount still owed.</p><p><strong>Cut-off.</strong> In practice a payable is booked after a <em>three-way match</em> of purchase order, receiving report and invoice, and a late invoice means a missed payable. For example (illustration), take the 803,062 that PHE owes third parties at the end of 2024 (Note 19) as the population, and suppose two items were missed at year end.</p><blockquote><p>Casing worth 2,000 shipped FOB shipping point on 29 December and invoiced on 30 December, invoice posted on 6 January: inventories and trade payables both understated by 2,000; profit unchanged</p><p>Well-servicing work worth 500 performed and invoiced in December, invoice posted in January: production expenses and trade payables understated by 500; profit overstated by 500</p><p>Correcting entry, 31 December: Dr Inventories 2,000, Dr Production expenses 500 / Cr Trade payables 2,500</p></blockquote><p>The first error only shrinks the balance sheet. The second flatters profit, which is why auditors search for unrecorded liabilities by reading payments made after year end. Had the invoices not yet arrived at year end, the same entry would credit accrued expenses instead (see the accrued expenses essay).</p><h2>3. Purchase discounts and the cost of not taking them</h2><p>A cash discount for prompt payment turns every payable into a small financing decision. The lecture notes record it in two ways. The <em>gross method</em> books the invoice amount and recognises the discount only when taken. The <em>net method</em> books the discounted amount, treating it as the real price, so a discount missed is shown as a finance cost (the inventories essay works both through to the cost of casing). For example (illustration), the lecture notes' purchase of 10,000 on terms of 2/10, net 30: a 2 percent discount if paid within 10 days, otherwise the full amount by day 30.</p><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Gross method</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Net method</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Purchase</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Inventories 10,000 / Cr Trade payables 10,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Inventories 9,800 / Cr Trade payables 9,800</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Paid on day 10</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Trade payables 10,000 / Cr Inventories 200 / Cr Cash 9,800</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Trade payables 9,800 / Cr Cash 9,800</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Paid on day 30</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Trade payables 10,000 / Cr Cash 10,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Trade payables 9,800, Dr Purchase discounts lost 200 / Cr Cash 10,000</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>The net method shows what the gross method hides. Paying on day 30 instead of day 10 borrows 9,800 for 20 days at a cost of 200.</p><blockquote><p>Rate for 20 days = 200 / 9,800 = 2.04%</p><p>Annual rate, simple = 2.04% × 365 / 20 = 37.2%; compounded = (1 + 2.04%)^(365/20) − 1 = 44.6%</p></blockquote><p>Any entity that can borrow below 37 percent a year should take the discount.</p><h2>4. Notes payable</h2><p>A note payable is a written promise to pay a set sum on a set date, here arising from an overdue account payable. The lecture notes give two kinds.</p><p><strong>Interest-bearing notes</strong> state an interest rate, and interest accrues with time. For example (illustration), on 31 October a foreign supplier of a PHE subsidiary (PHE owes foreign suppliers 189,687, Note 19) agrees to convert an overdue invoice of 10,000 into a six-month note at 8 percent a year.</p><blockquote><p>31 October: Dr Trade payables 10,000 / Cr Notes payable 10,000</p><p>31 December, two months of interest = 10,000 × 8% × 2/12 = 133.33: Dr Interest expense 133.33 / Cr Interest payable 133.33</p><p>30 April, maturity, interest for six months = 400.00: Dr Notes payable 10,000, Dr Interest payable 133.33, Dr Interest expense 266.67 / Cr Cash 10,400</p></blockquote><p><strong>Zero-interest-bearing notes</strong> state only the amount due at maturity, so the interest sits in the gap between that amount and the present value received. A note of 110,000 due in a year at a market rate of 10 percent is recorded at 100,000, and 10,000 of interest expense accrues over the year; the long-term bank loans essay works such notes through.</p><h2>5. Payables in a foreign currency</h2><p>A payable in a currency other than the entity's functional currency is a <em>monetary item</em>, a right or obligation to a fixed number of currency units. PSAK 221 <em>The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates</em> (formerly PSAK 10, IAS 21) records it at the spot rate on the transaction date (para. 21), retranslates it at the closing rate at each reporting date (para. 23), and takes the exchange differences to profit or loss (para. 28). PHE's functional currency is the US dollar for every entity except Elnusa, which uses the rupiah (Note 3r, Daftar 5/66, PDF p. 83). Rupiah trade payables were 518,011 at the end of 2024 (Note 19b). Those of the dollar entities are retranslated through profit or loss; Elnusa's are translated on consolidation, with the difference in other comprehensive income. Note 48 shows the same payables as Rp8,369,504 million (Daftar 5/225, PDF p. 242), which implies a closing rate of 8,369,504 / 518.011 = Rp16,157 per US dollar.</p><p>For example (illustration), a dollar-functional subsidiary receives a rupiah invoice for Rp32,000 million on 1 December 2024 when the rate is Rp15,800, and pays it on 15 January 2025 at Rp16,300.</p><blockquote><p>1 December: 32,000 / 15,800 × 1,000 = 2,025.32: Dr Production expenses 2,025.32 / Cr Trade payables 2,025.32</p><p>31 December at 16,157: 32,000 / 16,157 × 1,000 = 1,980.57; gain = 2,025.32 − 1,980.57 = 44.75: Dr Trade payables 44.75 / Cr Foreign exchange gain 44.75</p><p>15 January at 16,300: cash = 1,963.19; gain = 1,980.57 − 1,963.19 = 17.38: Dr Trade payables 1,980.57 / Cr Cash 1,963.19 / Cr Foreign exchange gain 17.38</p></blockquote><p>The expense stays at 2,025.32, the rate on the day of the service. The weaker rupiah cut the dollar cost of settlement by 62.13, split between the two years in which the rate moved.</p><h2>6. Related-party payables and offsetting</h2><p><strong>Related parties.</strong> A payable to a parent, a fellow subsidiary or another government-related entity is measured like any other payable, but PSAK 224 <em>Related Party Disclosures</em> (formerly PSAK 7, IAS 24) requires the relationship, the transactions and the outstanding balances to be disclosed, because the terms may not be those an independent supplier would set. Entities controlled by the same government may use a reduced disclosure for balances with one another (paras. 25–26). PHE discloses its related-party trade payables by counterparty (Note 44e, Daftar 5/185, PDF p. 202).</p><p><strong>Offsetting.</strong> A payable and a receivable with the same party are shown net only when the entity has a legally enforceable right of set-off and intends to settle net or simultaneously (PSAK 232, formerly PSAK 50, IAS 32, para. 42); the trade receivables essay works this through PHE's Pertamina balances. PHE settles those balances by offset but presents them gross, a receivable of 18,434 and a payable of 105,797 (Note 44c, Daftar 5/184, PDF p. 201).</p><h2>7. Supplier finance arrangements</h2><p>In a <em>supplier finance arrangement</em>, also called supply chain finance, payables finance or reverse factoring, one or more finance providers pay what an entity owes its suppliers, and the entity pays the provider on or after the date the suppliers are paid (PSAK 207, formerly PSAK 2, IAS 7, para. 44G). The supplier gets cash early, priced on the buyer's credit, and the buyer often gets longer to pay.</p><p>The IFRS Interpretations Committee's December 2020 agenda decision on reverse factoring answered three questions from existing standards.</p><ul><li><p><strong>Presentation.</strong> A liability stays in trade payables only if it is a liability to pay for goods or services, is invoiced or formally agreed with the supplier (PSAK 237.11(a)), and is part of the working capital used in the normal operating cycle (PSAK 201.70). Extra security given to the bank, or terms that differ materially from those of other payables, point to a separate line or to borrowings.</p></li><li><p><strong>Derecognition.</strong> If the obligation to the supplier is extinguished, the payable is derecognised and a new liability to the bank is recognised (section 8).</p></li><li><p><strong>Cash flows.</strong> These follow the classification. A payable that stays in working capital is settled through operating cash flows. If it has become a borrowing, the bank's payment to the supplier is a non-cash transaction (PSAK 207.43), and the later payment to the bank is a financing outflow.</p></li></ul><p>For example (illustration), a subsidiary owes a supplier 10,000 due in 60 days. A bank pays the supplier 9,900 on day 10, with the supplier bearing the 1 percent discount.</p><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Case A: entity pays the bank 10,000 on day 60, no security</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Case B: entity pays the bank 10,150 on day 150, guaranteed by the parent</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Nature of the liability</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>the same trade payable, now owed to the bank</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>a 140-day financing with interest and security</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Day 10</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>no entry, or Dr Trade payables / Cr Trade payables, supplier finance, 10,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Trade payables 10,000 / Cr Supplier finance borrowings 10,000</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Day 150</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Supplier finance borrowings 10,000, Dr Interest expense 150 / Cr Cash 10,150</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Cash flow statement</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>operating outflow 10,000 on day 60</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>no operating outflow; financing outflow 10,000 and interest 150 by policy</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>In case B operating cash flow is 10,000 higher than if the entity had paid the supplier itself, with nothing in its operations changed, which is why analysts asked for disclosure.</p><p><strong>The 2024 disclosures.</strong> Amendments to PSAK 207 and PSAK 107 <em>Financial Instruments: Disclosures</em> (formerly PSAK 60, IFRS 7), effective for annual periods beginning on or after 1 January 2024, require the terms of the arrangements; at the start and end of the period, the liabilities in them, the lines they sit in, and how much the providers have already paid to suppliers; the range of due dates against comparable payables outside the arrangements; and non-cash changes such as exchange differences (PSAK 207.44H). PSAK 107 brings supplier finance into the liquidity risk disclosures. PHE adopted the amendments in 2024 and reports no significant impact on its financial reporting (Note 3b, Daftar 5/31, PDF p. 48). It does not say whether it has any such arrangements. Yet Note 44e lists PT Bank Rakyat Indonesia (8,454) and PT Bank Mandiri (4,897) among related-party trade creditors, and a bank in trade payables is what a supplier finance arrangement or a usance letter of credit would produce. The absence of a PSAK 207.44H disclosure suggests these balances are immaterial or of another kind.</p><h2>8. Derecognition</h2><p>A payable leaves the balance sheet in one of two ways under PSAK 109.</p><p><strong>Extinguishment</strong> (para. 3.3.1). The obligation is discharged by payment, cancelled by the creditor, or expires. Waiting is not enough. For example (illustration), a subsidiary carries 300 owed to a supplier that has not chased it for three years. Unless the debt has been legally released or has expired under the law of the contract, the 300 stays. A write-back on the strength of silence overstates profit and understates liabilities.</p><p><strong>Substantial modification</strong> (paras. 3.3.2–3.3.3). A substantial change of terms is accounted for as extinguishing the old liability and recognising a new one at fair value, with the difference in profit or loss. The quantitative test is whether the new cash flows, discounted at the original effective interest rate, differ by at least 10 percent (para. B3.3.6; the long-term bank loans essay teaches it on a loan).</p><p>For example (illustration), a subsidiary owes a struggling contractor 10,000, overdue. They agree that the contractor will accept 8,500 payable in one year, without interest. The original payable carried no interest, so its effective rate is 0 percent, and the market rate for this subsidiary's one-year debt is 8 percent.</p><blockquote><p>Present value of new cash flows at the original rate = 8,500 / 1.00 = 8,500</p><p>Difference = (10,000 − 8,500) / 10,000 = 15% ≥ 10%: extinguishment</p><p>Fair value of the new liability = 8,500 / 1.08 = 7,870.37</p><p>Gain = 10,000 − 7,870.37 = 2,129.63</p><p>Dr Trade payables 10,000 / Cr Notes payable 7,870.37 / Cr Gain on extinguishment of payables 2,129.63</p><p>Over the year: Dr Interest expense 629.63 / Cr Notes payable 629.63, bringing it to 8,500</p></blockquote><p>Had the contractor accepted 9,500 instead, the difference would be 5 percent. The payable would stay, adjusted to 9,500 with a modification gain of 500.</p><h2>9. Trade payables across industries</h2><p><strong>Retail.</strong> Supermarkets often sell goods before paying for them, so days payable exceed the days goods sit on shelves plus the days customers take to pay, and working capital is negative. For example (illustration), days in inventory 40, days sales outstanding 2, days payable outstanding 60.</p><blockquote><p>Cash conversion cycle = 40 + 2 − 60 = −18 days</p></blockquote><p>Suppliers finance the business. Goods taken on <em>consignment</em> create no payable until the retailer sells them, because the supplier keeps control, and <em>supplier rebates</em> reduce the cost of inventory (PSAK 202.11).</p><p><strong>Construction.</strong> Contractors keep a <em>retention</em>, commonly 5 to 10 percent of each subcontractor's certified progress, until the work passes inspection. The retention is owed, with timing tied to completion. For example (illustration), a subcontractor certificate of 2,000 with 5 percent retention.</p><blockquote><p>Dr Contract costs 2,000 / Cr Trade payables 1,900 / Cr Retentions payable 100</p></blockquote><p><strong>Banks and insurers.</strong> A bank's main liabilities are customer deposits, at amortised cost under PSAK 109, and an insurer's claims payable sits inside insurance contract liabilities under PSAK 117 (IFRS 17). Neither has a large trade payables line.</p><p><strong>Islamic finance.</strong> A buyer that purchases an asset under <em>murabahah</em>, a sale at cost plus a disclosed margin with deferred payment, records a murabahah payable for the full agreed price, with the margin as deferred murabahah expense, shown as a deduction from the payable and amortised as the payable is settled (PSAK 402, formerly PSAK 102; STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED). For example (illustration), equipment costing 1,000 bought with a margin of 100 payable in one year.</p><blockquote><p>Dr Equipment 1,000, Dr Deferred murabahah expense 100 / Cr Murabahah payable 1,100</p></blockquote><p>The economics are those of the zero-interest note in section 4, with the cost of credit called a margin.</p><p><strong>Upstream oil and gas.</strong> Joint operations create their own payables between operator and partners (section 11).</p><h2>10. Presentation and analysis: PHE's Notes 19, 21 and 44</h2><p>PHE splits its payables by supplier and by currency (Note 19, Daftar 5/113, PDF p. 130; Note 21, Daftar 5/115–5/116, PDF pp. 132–133).</p><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>31 Dec 2024</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>31 Dec 2023</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Trade payables, related parties (Note 44e)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>288,760</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>312,718</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Trade payables, local suppliers</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>613,375</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>383,055</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Trade payables, foreign suppliers</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>189,687</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>187,915</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>Trade payables</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>1,091,822</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>883,688</strong></p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Of which in rupiah</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>518,011</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>413,080</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Other payables, related parties (Note 44f)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>155,090</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>168,204</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Other payables from subsidiaries' oil and gas operations</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>429,695</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>300,799</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Due to operators, cash call shortfalls</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>28,933</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>12,960</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>Other payables</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>613,718</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>481,963</strong></p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Trade payables rose 23.6 percent from the end of 2023. Third parties account for more than all of it (up 40.6 percent, mostly local suppliers), while related-party payables fell 7.7 percent. Rupiah payables were 47.4 percent of the line.</p><p><strong>Days payable outstanding.</strong> DPO measures how long an entity takes to pay its suppliers.</p><blockquote><p>DPO = average trade payables / purchases on credit × 365</p></blockquote><p>PHE does not disclose purchases, but its direct-method cash flow statement shows cash paid to suppliers of 5,117,737 in 2024 (Daftar 4, PDF p. 17), which can stand in for them.</p><blockquote><p>Average trade payables = (1,091,822 + 883,688) / 2 = 987,755</p><p>DPO ≈ 987,755 / 5,117,737 × 365 = 70 days</p><p>At year end: 1,091,822 / 5,117,737 × 365 = 78 days (2023: 883,688 / 4,640,992 × 365 = 69 days)</p></blockquote><p>The proxy is rough. Trade payables include suppliers of drilling and other capital work, whose payments sit in investing cash flows (purchase of oil and gas properties, 3,933,616 in 2024 and 3,948,075 in 2023). Adding them to the denominator gives about 44 days at year end (2023: 38). Both measures rise, so the slower payment at the end of 2024 does not depend on the proxy.</p><p><strong>Related parties.</strong> Related-party trade payables were 1.88 percent of total liabilities, led by PT Pertamina (Persero) 105,797, PT Pertamina Patra Niaga 73,164 and PT Perusahaan Gas Negara 30,891; the list also includes PT Bank Rakyat Indonesia 8,454, PT Bank Mandiri 4,897 and the Ministry of Finance 3,665 (Note 44e). Note 44e says these payables arise from drilling, gas processing and other oil and gas services, but Note 44c labels the whole 105,797 owed to Pertamina as "payables in relation to payment of cash calls", Pertamina's funding of the group's operations, offset against what it owes for sales. A liability for cash advanced is not a liability for goods or services invoiced by a supplier (PSAK 237.11(a)). The balance barely moved over the year (103,376 a year earlier), Note 49d describes "funding arrangements with Pertamina", and the cash flow statement reports Pertamina funding within operating activities, nil in 2024 and an outflow of 4,633 in 2023 (Daftar 4, PDF p. 17). This page reads the 105,797 as closer to a borrowing; PHE does not explain the classification. The two banks are related to PHE through banking services (Note 44a, Daftar 5/182, PDF p. 199), a point section 7 takes up.</p><p><strong>Maturity.</strong> PHE's liquidity table puts all trade and other payables in the "less than one year" band (Note 49d, Daftar 5/233, PDF p. 250).</p><p><strong>Other current liabilities</strong> of 224 (324) carry no note reference.</p><h2>11. Upstream note: cash calls and joint interest billings</h2><p>PHE's subsidiaries operate some working areas and are non-operators in others, and both roles create payables. A partner in a joint operation recognises its share of jointly incurred liabilities (PSAK 111, formerly PSAK 66, IFRS 11). PHE's policy for unitised fields says that as operator it recognises a payable to non-operators when the <em>cash calls</em>, requests for funds in advance, it has received exceed their share of revenues, costs, capital spending and liabilities, and a receivable when they fall short (Note 3y, Daftar 5/75, PDF p. 92). As non-operator, PHE classifies its proportional share of the operator's <em>joint interest billing</em> (JIB) reports, statements of actual spending, as other payables, 429,695 at the end of 2024, and owes operators 28,933 of cash calls not yet paid (Note 21). The same relationship runs the other way in advances: cash calls paid to operators ahead of spending are 76,196 (Note 15, Daftar 5/106, PDF p. 123). For example (illustration), a non-operating subsidiary with a 30 percent interest receives a JIB showing 5,000 of operating costs for December.</p><blockquote><p>Share = 5,000 × 30% = 1,500</p><p>Dr Production expenses 1,500 / Cr Other payables, JIB 1,500</p></blockquote><p>Note 21 gives the basis for putting them in other payables, that they come from "a proportional share of the Joint Interest Billing (JIB) report" sent by the operator.</p><h2>12. Related lines</h2><ul><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/accrued-expenses">accrued-expenses</a>: services received but not yet invoiced, before they become payables.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/decommissioning-site-restoration-provision">decommissioning-site-restoration-provision</a>: where uncertain obligations and contingencies are taught.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/trade-receivables-net">trade-receivables-net</a>: offsetting, worked through PHE's Pertamina balances.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/inventories-net">inventories-net</a>: the asset side of purchases on credit, and purchase discounts.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/advances-prepayments-current">advances-prepayments-current</a>: cash calls paid to operators in advance.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/other-taxes-receivable-current">other-taxes-payable</a>: VAT and withholding taxes on supplier invoices.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/long-term-bank-loans-non-current">long-term-bank-loans-non-current</a>: where a supplier finance liability that has become a borrowing would sit; zero-interest notes, the 10 percent test and the PSAK 201 covenant amendments.</p></li></ul><h3>Least-confident assertions</h3><ul><li><p>That the 105,797 owed to Pertamina for cash calls is closer to a borrowing than to a trade payable is this page's reading of Notes 44c, 44e and 49d and the cash flow statement against PSAK 237.11(a). PHE does not explain the classification, the two notes describe the same balances differently, and Pertamina funding is reported in operating cash flows.</p></li><li><p>Note 19's English text says PHE's trade payables "were denominated in US Dollars", while the Indonesian text, the governing version, says "berdominasi" (mostly) US dollars, and the currency table shows 47.4 percent in rupiah. This page follows the Indonesian text and the table.</p></li><li><p>DPO rests on proxies, because PHE does not disclose credit purchases. Cash paid to suppliers alone gives about 70 days on average (year end 69 to 78); adding payments for oil and gas properties gives 38 to 44 at year end. The level depends on the denominator; the rise does not.</p></li><li><p>Why PT Bank Rakyat Indonesia and PT Bank Mandiri (13,351 together, 1.2 percent of trade payables) appear among trade creditors is not disclosed; supplier finance or usance letters of credit are this page's hypotheses. The Ministry of Finance's 3,665 may be rent for state-owned assets (Note 44a lists asset lease services), but that is inference.</p></li><li><p>The murabahah buyer entries under PSAK 402 and the PSAK 117 placement of insurers' claims payable are from knowledge and were not checked against the PSAK text.</p></li></ul><h2>References</h2><ol><li><p>PT Pertamina Hulu Energi dan Entitas Anak, consolidated financial statements 31 December 2024 (audited, USD thousand): statement of financial position (Daftar 1/2, PDF p. 12); cash flow statement (Daftar 4, PDF p. 17); Note 3b new standards (Daftar 5/31, PDF p. 48); Note 3 financial liabilities and offsetting (Daftar 5/44–5/47, PDF pp. 61–64); Note 3r (Daftar 5/66, PDF p. 83); Note 3t (Daftar 5/70, PDF p. 87); Note 3y (Daftar 5/75, PDF p. 92); Note 15 (Daftar 5/106, PDF p. 123); Note 19 (Daftar 5/113, PDF p. 130); Note 21 (Daftar 5/115–5/116, PDF pp. 132–133); Note 44a, 44c, 44e, 44f (Daftar 5/182–5/186, PDF pp. 199–203); Note 48 (Daftar 5/225, PDF p. 242); Note 49d (Daftar 5/233, PDF p. 250). VERIFIED (PHE FS 2024, on disk).</p></li><li><p>Lecture notes this series follows, "Inter 2 – Chapter 13 – Current Liabilities, Provisions and Contingencies" (Rumpun Akuntansi Keuangan), part A: definition and characteristics of current liabilities, accounts payable and recognition on transfer of control, gross and net methods, interest-bearing and zero-interest-bearing notes. NOTES. Corrections: (i) the net-method figure for a 2 percent discount on 10,000 should be 9,800, not 9,980; (ii) the zero-interest note never recognises interest expense, because the 10,000 is debited at maturity to an "interest payable" that was never credited; (iii) the notes call the present value the "face value"; (iv) the notes define current liabilities by the one-year test only. Foreign-currency payables, offsetting, supplier finance and derecognition are not in the notes and follow Kieso (ref. 8) and the standards named.</p></li><li><p><em>Conceptual Framework for Financial Reporting</em> (IASB 2018; Indonesian <em>Kerangka Konseptual Pelaporan Keuangan</em>), para. 4.26. STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED (from knowledge).</p></li><li><p>PSAK 207 <em>Laporan Arus Kas</em> (IAS 7) paras. 43, 44F–44H and the 2023 amendments <em>Supplier Finance Arrangements</em> (with PSAK 107 / IFRS 7, effective 1 January 2024). Content VERIFIED (web, Commission Regulation (EU) 2024/1317 adopting the IAS 7 and IFRS 7 amendments, via lawplayer.com). Indonesian effective date VERIFIED (web, PwC Indonesia, <em>Practical Guide to PSAK 2024</em>; PHE Note 3b).</p></li><li><p>IFRS Interpretations Committee, agenda decision <em>Supply Chain Financing Arrangements—Reverse Factoring</em> (December 2020), including the presentation criteria (IAS 37.11(a), IAS 1.70), derecognition and cash flow analysis. VERIFIED (web, ifrs.org compilation of agenda decisions vol. 4; Forvis Mazars, "Reverse factoring – Guidance on the applicable requirements under IFRS", May 2021).</p></li><li><p>PSAK 109 <em>Instrumen Keuangan</em> (IFRS 9), paras. 3.3.1–3.3.3 and B3.3.6 (10 percent test). B3.3.6 VERIFIED (web, IFRS Foundation staff papers on fees in the 10 per cent test). Other paragraphs STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.</p></li><li><p>PSAK 201 (IAS 1) paras. 69–70; PSAK 237 (IAS 37) para. 11; PSAK 221 (IAS 21) paras. 21, 23, 28 (number VERIFIED: PHE Note 3b lists the PSAK 221 amendment); PSAK 224 (IAS 24) paras. 25–26; PSAK 232 (IAS 32) para. 42; PSAK 111 (IFRS 11); PSAK 202 (IAS 2) para. 11; PSAK 117 (IFRS 17); PSAK 402 (murabahah). PSAK 201.70 and 237.11 content VERIFIED (web, via ref. 5); PSAK 232.42 wording matches PHE's policy (ref. 1). Numbers follow the IAI renumbering rule (IFRS n → PSAK 100+n, IAS n → PSAK 200+n; sharia standards 1xx → 4xx); other content STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.</p></li><li><p>Kieso, Weygandt and Warfield, <em>Intermediate Accounting</em>, IFRS edition, ch. 13 <em>Current Liabilities, Provisions and Contingencies</em>, the chapter the lecture notes summarise. Not on disk; NOT VERIFIED.</p></li></ol>$c$,
       content_json = $j${"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"In brief","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A trade payable is the simplest liability on the balance sheet, for goods or services received and invoiced, with amount and date known. That certainty separates it from an accrual (received, not yet invoiced) and a provision (timing or amount uncertain).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Payables are recognised when control of the goods passes or the service is performed, measured at the invoice amount because discounting a short-term balance changes nothing material, and kept in current liabilities because they belong to the operating cycle.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A payable leaves the balance sheet only when the obligation is paid, cancelled or expires, or when its terms change substantially. Supplier finance arrangements test that rule. Since 2024 PSAK 207 and PSAK 107 require entities to disclose them so that users can see how much of \"trade payables\" a bank has already paid.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"horizontalRule"},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PT Pertamina Hulu Energi and subsidiaries (PHE) report trade payables of 1,091,822 at 31 December 2024 against 883,688 a year earlier, in USD thousand (PHE FS 2024, statement of financial position, Daftar 1/2, PDF p. 12; \"Daftar\" is the printed page). The line is 7.1 percent of total liabilities and 17.7 percent of current liabilities (5.3 and 14.7 percent a year earlier), and 3.6 percent of total assets (2.9 percent). Beside it PHE reports other payables of 613,718 (481,963) and other current liabilities of 224 (324), which this page also covers.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"After reading it a student should be able to say when a payable exists, record it through its life in journals, judge whether a supplier finance arrangement has turned it into a borrowing, and read a payables note.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"All amounts are in USD thousand unless stated. Figures from PHE's statements are cited to their note. Every other number is labelled an illustration.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1. What a liability is, and which liabilities are current","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A liability is a present obligation of the entity to transfer an economic resource as a result of past events (","type":"text"},{"text":"Conceptual Framework for Financial Reporting","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" 2018, para. 4.26). For a payable the past event is the delivery of goods or the performance of a service. An undelivered purchase order for drilling chemicals is no liability yet.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The lecture notes define a current liability as one settled within a year with a resulting cash outflow. The 2018 framework drops the probability language from the definition, so uncertainty about the outflow affects recognition and measurement, and PSAK 201 adds the operating cycle to the one-year test.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Current or non-current.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" PSAK 201 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Presentation of Financial Statements","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (formerly PSAK 1, IAS 1) classifies a liability as current if any of four tests holds: the entity expects to settle it in its normal operating cycle, holds it for trading, must settle it within twelve months after the reporting date, or has no right at that date to defer settlement for at least twelve months (para. 69). Trade payables and accruals for operating costs are part of the working capital used in the operating cycle, so they are current even when due more than twelve months later (para. 70). PHE's policy uses the twelve-month and operating cycle tests (Note 3t, Daftar 5/70, PDF p. 87). The 2024 amendments to PSAK 201 on covenants and the right to defer settlement bite mainly on loans; see the long-term bank loans essay.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Payable, accrual or provision.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" PSAK 237 (formerly PSAK 57, IAS 37) draws the line (para. 11). ","type":"text"},{"text":"Trade payables","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" are liabilities for goods or services received or supplied that have been invoiced or formally agreed with the supplier. ","type":"text"},{"text":"Accruals","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" are liabilities for goods or services received or supplied that have not been invoiced or formally agreed. ","type":"text"},{"text":"Provisions","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" are liabilities of uncertain timing or amount. For example (illustration), three items at a PHE subsidiary on 31 December 2024:","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Item","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"What is known","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Line","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Workover rig hire for November, invoiced 20 December, 1,800 due on 19 January","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"amount and date","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"trade payables","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Helicopter flying hours in December, logged at 240 but not yet invoiced","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"amount nearly certain, no invoice","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"accrued expenses","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A contractor's claim of 3,000 for standby days, disputed","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"amount and timing uncertain","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"provision or contingent liability","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The accrued expenses essay and the decommissioning provision essay own the second and third rows.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2. Recognising and measuring accounts payable","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A payable arises from a time lag between receiving goods or services and paying for them. The lecture notes tie recognition to the transfer of control. For goods that depends on the shipping terms. Under FOB (free on board) shipping point the payable arises when the seller hands the goods to the carrier, under FOB destination when they arrive (see the inventories essay). For a service the trigger is performance.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Initial measurement.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" A trade payable is a financial liability under PSAK 109 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Financial Instruments","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (formerly PSAK 71, IFRS 9) and is first measured at fair value. For a short-term payable with no stated interest, fair value is the invoice amount, because discounting 30 or 60 days of credit changes nothing material. PHE states its payables at their \"notional amounts, which approximate their fair values\" (Note 3, financial liabilities, Daftar 5/46, PDF p. 63) and reports that its trade payables are unsecured and non-interest bearing (Note 19, Daftar 5/113, PDF p. 130). Afterwards they are carried at amortised cost, which for a non-interest-bearing payable is the amount still owed.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cut-off.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" In practice a payable is booked after a ","type":"text"},{"text":"three-way match","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" of purchase order, receiving report and invoice, and a late invoice means a missed payable. For example (illustration), take the 803,062 that PHE owes third parties at the end of 2024 (Note 19) as the population, and suppose two items were missed at year end.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Casing worth 2,000 shipped FOB shipping point on 29 December and invoiced on 30 December, invoice posted on 6 January: inventories and trade payables both understated by 2,000; profit unchanged","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Well-servicing work worth 500 performed and invoiced in December, invoice posted in January: production expenses and trade payables understated by 500; profit overstated by 500","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Correcting entry, 31 December: Dr Inventories 2,000, Dr Production expenses 500 / Cr Trade payables 2,500","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The first error only shrinks the balance sheet. The second flatters profit, which is why auditors search for unrecorded liabilities by reading payments made after year end. Had the invoices not yet arrived at year end, the same entry would credit accrued expenses instead (see the accrued expenses essay).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"3. Purchase discounts and the cost of not taking them","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A cash discount for prompt payment turns every payable into a small financing decision. The lecture notes record it in two ways. The ","type":"text"},{"text":"gross method","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" books the invoice amount and recognises the discount only when taken. The ","type":"text"},{"text":"net method","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" books the discounted amount, treating it as the real price, so a discount missed is shown as a finance cost (the inventories essay works both through to the cost of casing). For example (illustration), the lecture notes' purchase of 10,000 on terms of 2/10, net 30: a 2 percent discount if paid within 10 days, otherwise the full amount by day 30.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Gross method","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Net method","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Purchase","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Inventories 10,000 / Cr Trade payables 10,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Inventories 9,800 / Cr Trade payables 9,800","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Paid on day 10","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Trade payables 10,000 / Cr Inventories 200 / Cr Cash 9,800","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Trade payables 9,800 / Cr Cash 9,800","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Paid on day 30","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Trade payables 10,000 / Cr Cash 10,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Trade payables 9,800, Dr Purchase discounts lost 200 / Cr Cash 10,000","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The net method shows what the gross method hides. Paying on day 30 instead of day 10 borrows 9,800 for 20 days at a cost of 200.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Rate for 20 days = 200 / 9,800 = 2.04%","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Annual rate, simple = 2.04% × 365 / 20 = 37.2%; compounded = (1 + 2.04%)^(365/20) − 1 = 44.6%","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Any entity that can borrow below 37 percent a year should take the discount.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"4. Notes payable","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A note payable is a written promise to pay a set sum on a set date, here arising from an overdue account payable. The lecture notes give two kinds.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Interest-bearing notes","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" state an interest rate, and interest accrues with time. For example (illustration), on 31 October a foreign supplier of a PHE subsidiary (PHE owes foreign suppliers 189,687, Note 19) agrees to convert an overdue invoice of 10,000 into a six-month note at 8 percent a year.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"31 October: Dr Trade payables 10,000 / Cr Notes payable 10,000","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"31 December, two months of interest = 10,000 × 8% × 2/12 = 133.33: Dr Interest expense 133.33 / Cr Interest payable 133.33","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"30 April, maturity, interest for six months = 400.00: Dr Notes payable 10,000, Dr Interest payable 133.33, Dr Interest expense 266.67 / Cr Cash 10,400","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Zero-interest-bearing notes","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" state only the amount due at maturity, so the interest sits in the gap between that amount and the present value received. A note of 110,000 due in a year at a market rate of 10 percent is recorded at 100,000, and 10,000 of interest expense accrues over the year; the long-term bank loans essay works such notes through.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"5. Payables in a foreign currency","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A payable in a currency other than the entity's functional currency is a ","type":"text"},{"text":"monetary item","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", a right or obligation to a fixed number of currency units. PSAK 221 ","type":"text"},{"text":"The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (formerly PSAK 10, IAS 21) records it at the spot rate on the transaction date (para. 21), retranslates it at the closing rate at each reporting date (para. 23), and takes the exchange differences to profit or loss (para. 28). PHE's functional currency is the US dollar for every entity except Elnusa, which uses the rupiah (Note 3r, Daftar 5/66, PDF p. 83). Rupiah trade payables were 518,011 at the end of 2024 (Note 19b). Those of the dollar entities are retranslated through profit or loss; Elnusa's are translated on consolidation, with the difference in other comprehensive income. Note 48 shows the same payables as Rp8,369,504 million (Daftar 5/225, PDF p. 242), which implies a closing rate of 8,369,504 / 518.011 = Rp16,157 per US dollar.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"For example (illustration), a dollar-functional subsidiary receives a rupiah invoice for Rp32,000 million on 1 December 2024 when the rate is Rp15,800, and pays it on 15 January 2025 at Rp16,300.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1 December: 32,000 / 15,800 × 1,000 = 2,025.32: Dr Production expenses 2,025.32 / Cr Trade payables 2,025.32","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"31 December at 16,157: 32,000 / 16,157 × 1,000 = 1,980.57; gain = 2,025.32 − 1,980.57 = 44.75: Dr Trade payables 44.75 / Cr Foreign exchange gain 44.75","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"15 January at 16,300: cash = 1,963.19; gain = 1,980.57 − 1,963.19 = 17.38: Dr Trade payables 1,980.57 / Cr Cash 1,963.19 / Cr Foreign exchange gain 17.38","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The expense stays at 2,025.32, the rate on the day of the service. The weaker rupiah cut the dollar cost of settlement by 62.13, split between the two years in which the rate moved.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"6. Related-party payables and offsetting","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Related parties.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" A payable to a parent, a fellow subsidiary or another government-related entity is measured like any other payable, but PSAK 224 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Related Party Disclosures","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (formerly PSAK 7, IAS 24) requires the relationship, the transactions and the outstanding balances to be disclosed, because the terms may not be those an independent supplier would set. Entities controlled by the same government may use a reduced disclosure for balances with one another (paras. 25–26). PHE discloses its related-party trade payables by counterparty (Note 44e, Daftar 5/185, PDF p. 202).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Offsetting.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" A payable and a receivable with the same party are shown net only when the entity has a legally enforceable right of set-off and intends to settle net or simultaneously (PSAK 232, formerly PSAK 50, IAS 32, para. 42); the trade receivables essay works this through PHE's Pertamina balances. PHE settles those balances by offset but presents them gross, a receivable of 18,434 and a payable of 105,797 (Note 44c, Daftar 5/184, PDF p. 201).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"7. Supplier finance arrangements","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"In a ","type":"text"},{"text":"supplier finance arrangement","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", also called supply chain finance, payables finance or reverse factoring, one or more finance providers pay what an entity owes its suppliers, and the entity pays the provider on or after the date the suppliers are paid (PSAK 207, formerly PSAK 2, IAS 7, para. 44G). The supplier gets cash early, priced on the buyer's credit, and the buyer often gets longer to pay.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The IFRS Interpretations Committee's December 2020 agenda decision on reverse factoring answered three questions from existing standards.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Presentation.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" A liability stays in trade payables only if it is a liability to pay for goods or services, is invoiced or formally agreed with the supplier (PSAK 237.11(a)), and is part of the working capital used in the normal operating cycle (PSAK 201.70). Extra security given to the bank, or terms that differ materially from those of other payables, point to a separate line or to borrowings.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Derecognition.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" If the obligation to the supplier is extinguished, the payable is derecognised and a new liability to the bank is recognised (section 8).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cash flows.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" These follow the classification. A payable that stays in working capital is settled through operating cash flows. If it has become a borrowing, the bank's payment to the supplier is a non-cash transaction (PSAK 207.43), and the later payment to the bank is a financing outflow.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"For example (illustration), a subsidiary owes a supplier 10,000 due in 60 days. A bank pays the supplier 9,900 on day 10, with the supplier bearing the 1 percent discount.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Case A: entity pays the bank 10,000 on day 60, no security","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Case B: entity pays the bank 10,150 on day 150, guaranteed by the parent","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Nature of the liability","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"the same trade payable, now owed to the bank","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"a 140-day financing with interest and security","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Day 10","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"no entry, or Dr Trade payables / Cr Trade payables, supplier finance, 10,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Trade payables 10,000 / Cr Supplier finance borrowings 10,000","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Day 150","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Supplier finance borrowings 10,000, Dr Interest expense 150 / Cr Cash 10,150","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cash flow statement","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"operating outflow 10,000 on day 60","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"no operating outflow; financing outflow 10,000 and interest 150 by policy","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"In case B operating cash flow is 10,000 higher than if the entity had paid the supplier itself, with nothing in its operations changed, which is why analysts asked for disclosure.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The 2024 disclosures.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Amendments to PSAK 207 and PSAK 107 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Financial Instruments: Disclosures","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (formerly PSAK 60, IFRS 7), effective for annual periods beginning on or after 1 January 2024, require the terms of the arrangements; at the start and end of the period, the liabilities in them, the lines they sit in, and how much the providers have already paid to suppliers; the range of due dates against comparable payables outside the arrangements; and non-cash changes such as exchange differences (PSAK 207.44H). PSAK 107 brings supplier finance into the liquidity risk disclosures. PHE adopted the amendments in 2024 and reports no significant impact on its financial reporting (Note 3b, Daftar 5/31, PDF p. 48). It does not say whether it has any such arrangements. Yet Note 44e lists PT Bank Rakyat Indonesia (8,454) and PT Bank Mandiri (4,897) among related-party trade creditors, and a bank in trade payables is what a supplier finance arrangement or a usance letter of credit would produce. The absence of a PSAK 207.44H disclosure suggests these balances are immaterial or of another kind.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"8. Derecognition","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A payable leaves the balance sheet in one of two ways under PSAK 109.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Extinguishment","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" (para. 3.3.1). The obligation is discharged by payment, cancelled by the creditor, or expires. Waiting is not enough. For example (illustration), a subsidiary carries 300 owed to a supplier that has not chased it for three years. Unless the debt has been legally released or has expired under the law of the contract, the 300 stays. A write-back on the strength of silence overstates profit and understates liabilities.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Substantial modification","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" (paras. 3.3.2–3.3.3). A substantial change of terms is accounted for as extinguishing the old liability and recognising a new one at fair value, with the difference in profit or loss. The quantitative test is whether the new cash flows, discounted at the original effective interest rate, differ by at least 10 percent (para. B3.3.6; the long-term bank loans essay teaches it on a loan).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"For example (illustration), a subsidiary owes a struggling contractor 10,000, overdue. They agree that the contractor will accept 8,500 payable in one year, without interest. The original payable carried no interest, so its effective rate is 0 percent, and the market rate for this subsidiary's one-year debt is 8 percent.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Present value of new cash flows at the original rate = 8,500 / 1.00 = 8,500","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Difference = (10,000 − 8,500) / 10,000 = 15% ≥ 10%: extinguishment","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Fair value of the new liability = 8,500 / 1.08 = 7,870.37","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Gain = 10,000 − 7,870.37 = 2,129.63","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Trade payables 10,000 / Cr Notes payable 7,870.37 / Cr Gain on extinguishment of payables 2,129.63","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Over the year: Dr Interest expense 629.63 / Cr Notes payable 629.63, bringing it to 8,500","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Had the contractor accepted 9,500 instead, the difference would be 5 percent. The payable would stay, adjusted to 9,500 with a modification gain of 500.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"9. Trade payables across industries","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Retail.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Supermarkets often sell goods before paying for them, so days payable exceed the days goods sit on shelves plus the days customers take to pay, and working capital is negative. For example (illustration), days in inventory 40, days sales outstanding 2, days payable outstanding 60.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cash conversion cycle = 40 + 2 − 60 = −18 days","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Suppliers finance the business. Goods taken on ","type":"text"},{"text":"consignment","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" create no payable until the retailer sells them, because the supplier keeps control, and ","type":"text"},{"text":"supplier rebates","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" reduce the cost of inventory (PSAK 202.11).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Construction.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Contractors keep a ","type":"text"},{"text":"retention","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", commonly 5 to 10 percent of each subcontractor's certified progress, until the work passes inspection. The retention is owed, with timing tied to completion. For example (illustration), a subcontractor certificate of 2,000 with 5 percent retention.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Contract costs 2,000 / Cr Trade payables 1,900 / Cr Retentions payable 100","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Banks and insurers.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" A bank's main liabilities are customer deposits, at amortised cost under PSAK 109, and an insurer's claims payable sits inside insurance contract liabilities under PSAK 117 (IFRS 17). Neither has a large trade payables line.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Islamic finance.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" A buyer that purchases an asset under ","type":"text"},{"text":"murabahah","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", a sale at cost plus a disclosed margin with deferred payment, records a murabahah payable for the full agreed price, with the margin as deferred murabahah expense, shown as a deduction from the payable and amortised as the payable is settled (PSAK 402, formerly PSAK 102; STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED). For example (illustration), equipment costing 1,000 bought with a margin of 100 payable in one year.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Equipment 1,000, Dr Deferred murabahah expense 100 / Cr Murabahah payable 1,100","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The economics are those of the zero-interest note in section 4, with the cost of credit called a margin.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Upstream oil and gas.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Joint operations create their own payables between operator and partners (section 11).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"10. Presentation and analysis: PHE's Notes 19, 21 and 44","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PHE splits its payables by supplier and by currency (Note 19, Daftar 5/113, PDF p. 130; Note 21, Daftar 5/115–5/116, PDF pp. 132–133).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"31 Dec 2024","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"31 Dec 2023","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Trade payables, related parties (Note 44e)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"288,760","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"312,718","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Trade payables, local suppliers","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"613,375","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"383,055","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Trade payables, foreign suppliers","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"189,687","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"187,915","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Trade payables","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1,091,822","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"883,688","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Of which in rupiah","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"518,011","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"413,080","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Other payables, related parties (Note 44f)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"155,090","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"168,204","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Other payables from subsidiaries' oil and gas operations","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"429,695","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"300,799","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Due to operators, cash call shortfalls","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"28,933","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"12,960","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Other payables","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"613,718","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"481,963","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Trade payables rose 23.6 percent from the end of 2023. Third parties account for more than all of it (up 40.6 percent, mostly local suppliers), while related-party payables fell 7.7 percent. Rupiah payables were 47.4 percent of the line.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Days payable outstanding.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" DPO measures how long an entity takes to pay its suppliers.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"DPO = average trade payables / purchases on credit × 365","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PHE does not disclose purchases, but its direct-method cash flow statement shows cash paid to suppliers of 5,117,737 in 2024 (Daftar 4, PDF p. 17), which can stand in for them.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Average trade payables = (1,091,822 + 883,688) / 2 = 987,755","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"DPO ≈ 987,755 / 5,117,737 × 365 = 70 days","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"At year end: 1,091,822 / 5,117,737 × 365 = 78 days (2023: 883,688 / 4,640,992 × 365 = 69 days)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The proxy is rough. Trade payables include suppliers of drilling and other capital work, whose payments sit in investing cash flows (purchase of oil and gas properties, 3,933,616 in 2024 and 3,948,075 in 2023). Adding them to the denominator gives about 44 days at year end (2023: 38). Both measures rise, so the slower payment at the end of 2024 does not depend on the proxy.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Related parties.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Related-party trade payables were 1.88 percent of total liabilities, led by PT Pertamina (Persero) 105,797, PT Pertamina Patra Niaga 73,164 and PT Perusahaan Gas Negara 30,891; the list also includes PT Bank Rakyat Indonesia 8,454, PT Bank Mandiri 4,897 and the Ministry of Finance 3,665 (Note 44e). Note 44e says these payables arise from drilling, gas processing and other oil and gas services, but Note 44c labels the whole 105,797 owed to Pertamina as \"payables in relation to payment of cash calls\", Pertamina's funding of the group's operations, offset against what it owes for sales. A liability for cash advanced is not a liability for goods or services invoiced by a supplier (PSAK 237.11(a)). The balance barely moved over the year (103,376 a year earlier), Note 49d describes \"funding arrangements with Pertamina\", and the cash flow statement reports Pertamina funding within operating activities, nil in 2024 and an outflow of 4,633 in 2023 (Daftar 4, PDF p. 17). This page reads the 105,797 as closer to a borrowing; PHE does not explain the classification. The two banks are related to PHE through banking services (Note 44a, Daftar 5/182, PDF p. 199), a point section 7 takes up.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Maturity.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" PHE's liquidity table puts all trade and other payables in the \"less than one year\" band (Note 49d, Daftar 5/233, PDF p. 250).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Other current liabilities","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" of 224 (324) carry no note reference.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"11. Upstream note: cash calls and joint interest billings","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PHE's subsidiaries operate some working areas and are non-operators in others, and both roles create payables. A partner in a joint operation recognises its share of jointly incurred liabilities (PSAK 111, formerly PSAK 66, IFRS 11). PHE's policy for unitised fields says that as operator it recognises a payable to non-operators when the ","type":"text"},{"text":"cash calls","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", requests for funds in advance, it has received exceed their share of revenues, costs, capital spending and liabilities, and a receivable when they fall short (Note 3y, Daftar 5/75, PDF p. 92). As non-operator, PHE classifies its proportional share of the operator's ","type":"text"},{"text":"joint interest billing","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (JIB) reports, statements of actual spending, as other payables, 429,695 at the end of 2024, and owes operators 28,933 of cash calls not yet paid (Note 21). The same relationship runs the other way in advances: cash calls paid to operators ahead of spending are 76,196 (Note 15, Daftar 5/106, PDF p. 123). For example (illustration), a non-operating subsidiary with a 30 percent interest receives a JIB showing 5,000 of operating costs for December.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Share = 5,000 × 30% = 1,500","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Production expenses 1,500 / Cr Other payables, JIB 1,500","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Note 21 gives the basis for putting them in other payables, that they come from \"a proportional share of the Joint Interest Billing (JIB) report\" sent by the operator.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"12. Related lines","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"accrued-expenses","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/accrued-expenses","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": services received but not yet invoiced, before they become payables.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"decommissioning-site-restoration-provision","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/decommissioning-site-restoration-provision","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": where uncertain obligations and contingencies are taught.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"trade-receivables-net","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/trade-receivables-net","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": offsetting, worked through PHE's Pertamina balances.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"inventories-net","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/inventories-net","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": the asset side of purchases on credit, and purchase discounts.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"advances-prepayments-current","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/advances-prepayments-current","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": cash calls paid to operators in advance.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"other-taxes-payable","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/other-taxes-receivable-current","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": VAT and withholding taxes on supplier invoices.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"long-term-bank-loans-non-current","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/long-term-bank-loans-non-current","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": where a supplier finance liability that has become a borrowing would sit; zero-interest notes, the 10 percent test and the PSAK 201 covenant amendments.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Least-confident assertions","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"That the 105,797 owed to Pertamina for cash calls is closer to a borrowing than to a trade payable is this page's reading of Notes 44c, 44e and 49d and the cash flow statement against PSAK 237.11(a). PHE does not explain the classification, the two notes describe the same balances differently, and Pertamina funding is reported in operating cash flows.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Note 19's English text says PHE's trade payables \"were denominated in US Dollars\", while the Indonesian text, the governing version, says \"berdominasi\" (mostly) US dollars, and the currency table shows 47.4 percent in rupiah. This page follows the Indonesian text and the table.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"DPO rests on proxies, because PHE does not disclose credit purchases. Cash paid to suppliers alone gives about 70 days on average (year end 69 to 78); adding payments for oil and gas properties gives 38 to 44 at year end. The level depends on the denominator; the rise does not.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Why PT Bank Rakyat Indonesia and PT Bank Mandiri (13,351 together, 1.2 percent of trade payables) appear among trade creditors is not disclosed; supplier finance or usance letters of credit are this page's hypotheses. The Ministry of Finance's 3,665 may be rent for state-owned assets (Note 44a lists asset lease services), but that is inference.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The murabahah buyer entries under PSAK 402 and the PSAK 117 placement of insurers' claims payable are from knowledge and were not checked against the PSAK text.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"References","type":"text"}]},{"type":"orderedList","attrs":{"type":null,"start":1},"content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PT Pertamina Hulu Energi dan Entitas Anak, consolidated financial statements 31 December 2024 (audited, USD thousand): statement of financial position (Daftar 1/2, PDF p. 12); cash flow statement (Daftar 4, PDF p. 17); Note 3b new standards (Daftar 5/31, PDF p. 48); Note 3 financial liabilities and offsetting (Daftar 5/44–5/47, PDF pp. 61–64); Note 3r (Daftar 5/66, PDF p. 83); Note 3t (Daftar 5/70, PDF p. 87); Note 3y (Daftar 5/75, PDF p. 92); Note 15 (Daftar 5/106, PDF p. 123); Note 19 (Daftar 5/113, PDF p. 130); Note 21 (Daftar 5/115–5/116, PDF pp. 132–133); Note 44a, 44c, 44e, 44f (Daftar 5/182–5/186, PDF pp. 199–203); Note 48 (Daftar 5/225, PDF p. 242); Note 49d (Daftar 5/233, PDF p. 250). VERIFIED (PHE FS 2024, on disk).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Lecture notes this series follows, \"Inter 2 – Chapter 13 – Current Liabilities, Provisions and Contingencies\" (Rumpun Akuntansi Keuangan), part A: definition and characteristics of current liabilities, accounts payable and recognition on transfer of control, gross and net methods, interest-bearing and zero-interest-bearing notes. NOTES. Corrections: (i) the net-method figure for a 2 percent discount on 10,000 should be 9,800, not 9,980; (ii) the zero-interest note never recognises interest expense, because the 10,000 is debited at maturity to an \"interest payable\" that was never credited; (iii) the notes call the present value the \"face value\"; (iv) the notes define current liabilities by the one-year test only. Foreign-currency payables, offsetting, supplier finance and derecognition are not in the notes and follow Kieso (ref. 8) and the standards named.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Conceptual Framework for Financial Reporting","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (IASB 2018; Indonesian ","type":"text"},{"text":"Kerangka Konseptual Pelaporan Keuangan","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":"), para. 4.26. STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED (from knowledge).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 207 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Laporan Arus Kas","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (IAS 7) paras. 43, 44F–44H and the 2023 amendments ","type":"text"},{"text":"Supplier Finance Arrangements","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (with PSAK 107 / IFRS 7, effective 1 January 2024). Content VERIFIED (web, Commission Regulation (EU) 2024/1317 adopting the IAS 7 and IFRS 7 amendments, via lawplayer.com). Indonesian effective date VERIFIED (web, PwC Indonesia, ","type":"text"},{"text":"Practical Guide to PSAK 2024","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":"; PHE Note 3b).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"IFRS Interpretations Committee, agenda decision ","type":"text"},{"text":"Supply Chain Financing Arrangements—Reverse Factoring","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (December 2020), including the presentation criteria (IAS 37.11(a), IAS 1.70), derecognition and cash flow analysis. VERIFIED (web, ifrs.org compilation of agenda decisions vol. 4; Forvis Mazars, \"Reverse factoring – Guidance on the applicable requirements under IFRS\", May 2021).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 109 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Instrumen Keuangan","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (IFRS 9), paras. 3.3.1–3.3.3 and B3.3.6 (10 percent test). B3.3.6 VERIFIED (web, IFRS Foundation staff papers on fees in the 10 per cent test). Other paragraphs STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 201 (IAS 1) paras. 69–70; PSAK 237 (IAS 37) para. 11; PSAK 221 (IAS 21) paras. 21, 23, 28 (number VERIFIED: PHE Note 3b lists the PSAK 221 amendment); PSAK 224 (IAS 24) paras. 25–26; PSAK 232 (IAS 32) para. 42; PSAK 111 (IFRS 11); PSAK 202 (IAS 2) para. 11; PSAK 117 (IFRS 17); PSAK 402 (murabahah). PSAK 201.70 and 237.11 content VERIFIED (web, via ref. 5); PSAK 232.42 wording matches PHE's policy (ref. 1). Numbers follow the IAI renumbering rule (IFRS n → PSAK 100+n, IAS n → PSAK 200+n; sharia standards 1xx → 4xx); other content STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Kieso, Weygandt and Warfield, ","type":"text"},{"text":"Intermediate Accounting","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", IFRS edition, ch. 13 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Current Liabilities, Provisions and Contingencies","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", the chapter the lecture notes summarise. Not on disk; NOT VERIFIED.","type":"text"}]}]}]}]}$j$::jsonb,
       presentation = $p${"deck":"The theory of accounts payable from the definition of a liability to supplier finance arrangements, worked through PHE's payables to suppliers, partners and the Pertamina group and set beside the industries where payables behave differently","genre":null,"author_bio":null,"references":[],"hero_caption":null,"key_takeaways":["A trade payable is the simplest liability on the balance sheet, for goods or services received and invoiced, with amount and date known. That certainty separates it from an accrual (received, not yet invoiced) and a provision (timing or amount uncertain).","Payables are recognised when control of the goods passes or the service is performed, measured at the invoice amount because discounting a short-term balance changes nothing material, and kept in current liabilities because they belong to the operating cycle.","A payable leaves the balance sheet only when the obligation is paid, cancelled or expires, or when its terms change substantially. Supplier finance arrangements test that rule. Since 2024 PSAK 207 and PSAK 107 require entities to disclose them so that users can see how much of \"trade payables\" a bank has already paid."],"revision_note":null}$p$::jsonb,
       updated_at = now()
 WHERE id = 'a1561fc6-765d-4fc0-84b1-a5da1a890033';
INSERT INTO public.essay_revisions (essay_id, revision_no, change_type, title, snippet, content_json, status, change_summary, changed_by)
SELECT e.id, coalesce((SELECT max(x.revision_no) FROM public.essay_revisions x WHERE x.essay_id = e.id), 0) + 1, 'rollback',
       e.title, e.snippet, e.content_json, e.status, $s$Restored from the snapshot taken before the lecture-notes removal$s$, null
  FROM public.essays e WHERE e.id = 'a1561fc6-765d-4fc0-84b1-a5da1a890033';

-- trade-receivables-net-recognition-ecl-derecognition
UPDATE public.essays
   SET content = $c$<p><strong>In brief</strong></p><ul><li><p>A trade receivable is born with revenue, at the moment control of goods or services passes to a customer and the seller's right to payment depends only on time. Revenue is measured at the PSAK 115 transaction price, net of trade discounts and of the discounts, returns and financing the seller expects, and the receivable records the unconditional right to payment.</p></li><li><p>The "net" in the line is an allowance for expected credit losses (ECL) under PSAK 109. Trade receivables without a significant financing component must use the simplified approach, lifetime losses from day one, usually through a provision matrix of loss rates by days past due adjusted for forecasts. The old incurred-loss model, which waited for a loss event, is gone.</p></li><li><p>A receivable leaves the balance sheet when it is collected, written off because recovery is no longer reasonably expected, or transferred with substantially all its risks and rewards. Factoring without recourse is a sale; factoring with recourse usually stays on the balance sheet as a secured borrowing.</p></li></ul><hr><p>PT Pertamina Hulu Energi and subsidiaries (PHE) report trade receivables, net, of 3,146,868 at 31 December 2024 against 2,768,397 a year earlier, in USD thousand (PHE FS 2024, statement of financial position, Daftar 1/1, PDF p. 11; "Daftar" is the printed page). The line is 10.3 percent of total assets and 39.2 percent of current assets (9.0 and 28.0 percent a year earlier), the largest current asset PHE has. This page uses those numbers as the starting point for its examples.</p><p>After reading it a student should be able to say when a receivable arises, measure it at recognition and at the reporting date, record write-offs, recoveries and transfers, and see where banks, Islamic financiers, builders and miners depart from the plain model.</p><p>All amounts are in USD thousand unless stated. Figures from PHE's statements are cited to their note. Every other number is labelled an illustration.</p><h2>1. What a trade receivable is</h2><p>A receivable is a claim on someone else for cash. Kieso sorts claims three ways. <em>Trade receivables</em> arise from selling goods or services in the ordinary course of business; <em>nontrade receivables</em> arise from anything else, such as insurance claims, advances to employees or deposits. <em>Accounts receivable</em> are open-account promises with no formal instrument; <em>notes receivable</em> are written promises to pay a sum at a date, usually with interest. And receivables are <em>current</em> if collection is expected within the operating cycle or twelve months, <em>non-current</em> otherwise (PSAK 201, formerly PSAK 1, IAS 1).</p><p>PHE's policy uses the first split. Trade receivables are amounts due from customers for crude oil, natural gas, drilling services, oil and gas support services, and energy distribution and logistics services; other receivables are amounts from third or related parties "beyond the ordinary course of business" (Note 3h, Daftar 5/51, PDF p. 68). Collection within one period puts a receivable in current assets, otherwise non-current (same note). PHE's separate "long term receivables" of 2,087 (2023: 2,997) carry no note reference on the face.</p><p>PSAK 115 (formerly PSAK 72, IFRS 15) adds a distinction that matters most for builders. A <em>receivable</em> is a right to consideration that is unconditional, meaning only the passage of time is required before payment falls due (para. 108). A <em>contract asset</em> is a right that still depends on something other than time, typically further performance.</p><h2>2. Recognition: the receivable follows revenue</h2><p>A receivable appears in the same journal entry as revenue, so "when do we record it?" is the revenue question. Kieso's <em>Intermediate Accounting</em> sets out the five steps of PSAK 115. Identify the contract, identify the performance obligations, determine the transaction price, allocate it, and recognise revenue when each obligation is satisfied, that is, when the customer obtains control.</p><p>For a crude seller, control typically passes at lifting, on FOB terms when the cargo crosses the custody point into the buyer's tanker. PHE recognises crude and gas revenue "based on the actual lifting amount and sold to customers" (Note 3p, Daftar 5/62, PDF p. 79). For example (illustration), a PHE subsidiary lifts 600 thousand barrels for a domestic refinery at USD 75 per barrel, payable in 30 days.</p><blockquote><p>Invoice = 600 × 75 = 45,000</p></blockquote><blockquote><p>Dr Trade receivables 45,000 / Cr Revenue from crude oil 45,000</p></blockquote><p>There is no matching "Dr Cost of goods sold / Cr Inventory" of the kind a merchandiser records. Note 11 lists only materials, and Note 3p charges the related costs on the amount actually sold, so the cost of producing the barrels has already gone to expense.</p><p>Signing a contract creates no receivable. Until delivery each side holds only an unperformed promise, and the two cancel out.</p><h2>3. Measurement at recognition</h2><p>The receivable starts at the transaction price, the consideration the seller expects to be entitled to (PSAK 115.47). Four things can make that differ from the list price.</p><p><strong>Trade discounts</strong> are price reductions given to a class of customer. They never reach the books, so a list price of 80 per barrel less a 6.25 percent volume discount is recorded at 75.</p><p><strong>Cash discounts</strong> reward prompt payment. Kieso gives two methods, mirror images of the purchase methods in the inventories essay. The <em>gross method</em> records the full invoice and the discount only when taken; the <em>net method</em> records the discounted amount and shows a discount the customer forfeits as income. For example (illustration), a PHE drilling subsidiary bills 2,000 on terms of 2/10, net 30, so prompt payment saves the customer 2,000 × 2% = 40.</p><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Gross method</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Net method</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Invoice</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Trade receivables 2,000 / Cr Service revenue 2,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Trade receivables 1,960 / Cr Service revenue 1,960</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Paid within 10 days</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Cash 1,960, Dr Sales discounts 40 / Cr Trade receivables 2,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Cash 1,960 / Cr Trade receivables 1,960</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Paid late</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Cash 2,000 / Cr Trade receivables 2,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Cash 2,000 / Cr Trade receivables 1,960, Cr Sales discounts forfeited 40</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>PSAK 115 replaces this choice with an estimate. A prompt-payment discount is <em>variable consideration</em>, so the seller includes in revenue the amount it expects, using the expected value or the most likely amount. If 70 percent of such customers take the discount:</p><blockquote><p>Expected transaction price = 2,000 − 70% × 40 = 1,972</p></blockquote><p>A common treatment keeps the receivable at the invoice amount of 2,000, which is what the customer owes if it pays late, and holds the expected discount of 28 as a refund liability.</p><p><strong>Sales returns and allowances</strong> follow the same logic. A crude cargo rarely comes back, but price allowances for off-specification quality do happen. If a buyer is expected to claim 1 percent on the 45,000 cargo in section 2, revenue is 44,550 and the 450 sits as a refund liability (or as a credit to the receivable once agreed).</p><blockquote><p>Dr Revenue from crude oil (allowance) 450 / Cr Refund liability 450</p></blockquote><p><strong>Time value.</strong> When payment comes long after delivery, part of what the customer pays is interest, and PSAK 115 takes it out of revenue (paras. 60–65). An entity may ignore it when it expects at contract inception that the gap will be one year or less (para. 63), which is why a 30-day crude invoice is never discounted. The lecture notes this series follows adapt Kieso's long case to a crude cargo sold on 1 July 2019 for a four-year zero-interest note when the market rate is 12 percent. Revenue is the cash price of 1,000,000, the note's face value is 1,000,000 × 1.57352 (the four-year factor at 12 percent) = 1,573,520, and interest is 1,000,000 × 12% × 6/12 = 60,000 for 2019 (accrued at 31 December) and 1,060,000 × 12% = 127,200 for 2020.</p><p>For example (illustration), a PHE subsidiary bills 10,500 for a drilling campaign and agrees that the customer pays three annual instalments of 3,500 starting a year after completion on 31 December 2024, with no interest. The customer would pay 8 percent a year to borrow.</p><blockquote><p>PV of 2025 instalment = 3,500 / 1.08 = 3,240.74</p><p>PV of 2026 instalment = 3,500 / 1.08² = 3,000.69</p><p>PV of 2027 instalment = 3,500 / 1.08³ = 2,778.41</p><p>Revenue and receivable at 31 Dec 2024 = 9,019.84; interest to be earned = 10,500 − 9,019.84 = 1,480.16</p></blockquote><table style="min-width: 125px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Year</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Opening</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Interest income at 8%</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Instalment</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Closing</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2025</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>9,019.84</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>721.59</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(3,500.00)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>6,241.43</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2026</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>6,241.43</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>499.31</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(3,500.00)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>3,240.74</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2027</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>3,240.74</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>259.26</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(3,500.00)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>0.00</p></td></tr></tbody></table><blockquote><p>31 Dec 2024: Dr Trade receivables 9,019.84 / Cr Service revenue 9,019.84</p><p>31 Dec 2025: Dr Trade receivables 721.59 / Cr Interest income 721.59; Dr Cash 3,500 / Cr Trade receivables 3,500</p></blockquote><p>The current/non-current boundary runs through this receivable. One method puts the present value of the 2025 instalment, 3,240.74, in current assets and 5,779.10 in non-current. The other puts in current assets only the fall in carrying amount during 2025, 9,019.84 − 6,241.43 = 2,778.41, leaving 6,241.43 non-current. PSAK 201 prescribes neither; the choice must be applied consistently.</p><h2>4. Subsequent measurement: amortised cost</h2><p>PSAK 109 (formerly PSAK 71, IFRS 9) classifies a financial asset by two tests: the business model (is it held to collect contractual cash flows?) and the cash flows (are they solely payments of principal and interest, SPPI?). A trade receivable passes both, so it is measured at <em>amortised cost</em>: the initial amount, plus interest at the effective interest rate (EIR), less cash collected, less the loss allowance. For a 30-day invoice the EIR is effectively nil, so amortised cost is the invoice less cash and allowance. For the instalment receivable above it is the schedule.</p><p>PHE's policy says trade and other receivables are "recognized initially at fair value and subsequently measured at amortized cost using the effective interest method, less provision for impairment" (Note 3h). PSAK 109 actually starts trade receivables without a significant financing component at the transaction price, which for short invoices is the same number.</p><h2>5. Expected credit losses</h2><p><strong>Why the model changed.</strong> Under IAS 39 an asset was written down only when there was objective evidence that it was impaired. That was the <em>incurred-loss</em> model. It waited for a loss event, a missed payment or a bankruptcy, so allowances arrived late, typically after the recession that caused them. PSAK 109 replaced it with <em>expected credit losses</em>, the probability-weighted present value of cash shortfalls, recognised before any default. Kieso's ageing schedule survives as the provision matrix.</p><p><strong>Two approaches.</strong> The <em>general approach</em> has three stages. A new asset carries 12-month ECL (stage 1). After a significant increase in credit risk, presumed when payments are more than 30 days past due, it carries lifetime ECL (stage 2). When it is credit-impaired it carries lifetime ECL and earns interest only on the net amount (stage 3); default is presumed no later than 90 days past due. Both presumptions are rebuttable with reasonable and supportable evidence. The <em>simplified approach</em> skips the tracking and recognises lifetime ECL from day one. It is required for trade receivables and contract assets without a significant financing component and is a policy choice for those with one, and for lease receivables (PSAK 109.5.5.15).</p><p>PHE applies the simplified approach because its receivables "do not contain significant financing component" (Note 3g, Daftar 5/43, PDF p. 60).</p><p><strong>The provision matrix.</strong> A matrix groups receivables that share credit risk, ages each group by days past due, and applies a loss rate to each bucket, drawn from history and adjusted for forecasts (PHE Note 4b(vi), Daftar 5/84, PDF p. 101). For example (illustration), PHE's third-party gross receivables of 536,508 at the end of 2024 (Note 8a) are split into five buckets. The split is assumed; PHE ages only the total.</p><table style="min-width: 125px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Days past due</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Gross</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Historical rate</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Adjusted rate (× 1.10)</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Allowance</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Not past due</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>380,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>0.30%</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>0.33%</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>1,254.00</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>1–90</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>120,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>1.50%</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>1.65%</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>1,980.00</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>91–180</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>6,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>10.00%</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>11.00%</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>660.00</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>181–365</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>18,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>25.00%</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>27.50%</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>4,950.00</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Over 365</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>12,508</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>60.00%</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>66.00%</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>8,255.28</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>Total</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>536,508</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>17,099.28</strong></p></td></tr></tbody></table><blockquote><p>At historical rates = 1,140.00 + 1,800.00 + 600.00 + 4,500.00 + 7,504.80 = 15,544.80</p><p>Forward-looking adjustment = 17,099.28 − 15,544.80 = 1,554.48</p></blockquote><p>The adjustment needs a reason that links a forecast to customers' ability to pay. PHE names gross domestic product as the most relevant factor and adjusts historical loss rates for its expected change (Note 49c, Daftar 5/229, PDF p. 246). Here the illustration assumes that a forecast one-point fall in GDP growth has, in past cycles, raised loss rates by a tenth. Current receivables carry an allowance too, which the incurred-loss model rarely gave them.</p><p><strong>Grouping matters.</strong> A matrix is valid only for receivables that share credit characteristics. PHE's related-party receivables of 2,627,769 are owed mostly by state-owned companies. Applying the third-party average rate (17,099.28 / 536,508 = 3.19 percent) to them would give an allowance of about 83,751. A rate built from the debtor's own probability of default (PD) and loss given default (LGD), which PHE says its models use (Note 49c), gives a much smaller number. Assume a one-year PD of 0.3 percent, LGD of 40 percent and an average time to collection of 60 days:</p><blockquote><p>2,627,769 × 0.3% × 40% × 60 / 365 = 518.35</p></blockquote><p><strong>Recording.</strong> The allowance is a contra account, so the ledger keeps each customer at the gross amount. Starting from PHE's opening third-party allowance of 15,815 (Note 8a, 2023):</p><blockquote><p>Charge for the year = 17,099.28 − 15,815.00 = 1,284.28</p><p>Dr Impairment loss on trade receivables 1,284.28 / Cr Allowance for expected credit losses 1,284.28</p></blockquote><h2>6. Write-offs and recoveries</h2><p>A write-off removes the part of a receivable the entity has no reasonable expectation of recovering (PSAK 109.5.4.4; PHE Note 3g, Daftar 5/44, PDF p. 61). Under the allowance method it does not touch profit, because the loss was expensed when the allowance was built. For example (illustration), a third-party gas customer owing 1,200 is declared bankrupt with nothing for unsecured creditors.</p><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Event</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Allowance method</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Direct write-off method</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Write-off</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Allowance 1,200 / Cr Trade receivables 1,200</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Bad debt expense 1,200 / Cr Trade receivables 1,200</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>300 recovered later</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Trade receivables 300 / Cr Allowance 300; Dr Cash 300 / Cr Trade receivables 300</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Cash 300 / Cr Bad debt recovery 300</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Kieso keeps the direct method for illustration only. It puts the expense in the year a customer fails instead of the year of the sale, and it carries the receivable at more than will be collected. PSAK 109 does not permit it.</p><p>The year's expense is whatever brings the allowance to its required balance after write-offs and recoveries.</p><blockquote><p>Impairment expense = closing allowance − opening allowance + write-offs − recoveries</p></blockquote><p>PHE's 2024 movement has no write-offs. It runs from 92,346 at the start of the year, less a reversal of 57,813 and a foreign exchange deduction of 17,124, to 17,409 (Note 8d, Daftar 5/96, PDF p. 113). The reversal appears as "Impairment and correction of receivables" of 57,813 in other income (Note 42, Daftar 5/150, PDF p. 167), so 2024 profit gained that amount from releasing allowances.</p><h2>7. Derecognition, factoring and offsetting</h2><p>A receivable can be turned into cash before it is due. Kieso describes two routes. <em>Assignment or pledging</em> uses receivables as security for a loan; the receivables stay on the books and the loan is a liability. <em>Factoring</em> sells them to a finance company, the factor, which then collects from the customers.</p><p>Whether a factoring is a sale turns on PSAK 109's derecognition tests. An asset is derecognised when the rights to its cash flows expire, or when they are transferred together with substantially all the risks and rewards of ownership. If the seller keeps substantially all the risks, typically by guaranteeing the factor against bad debts (<em>recourse</em>), the asset stays on the balance sheet and the cash is a borrowing. If risks are neither substantially transferred nor kept, the asset goes if the buyer controls it and stays to the extent of continuing involvement if not (PHE Note 3g, Daftar 5/42, PDF p. 59, summarises the same tests).</p><p>For example (illustration), a subsidiary factors 20,000 of gas receivables. The factor charges 2 percent and holds back 5 percent, 1,000, against disputes, so it pays 18,600 now.</p><table style="min-width: 50px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Without recourse (sale)</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>With recourse (secured borrowing)</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Cash 18,600, Dr Due from factor 1,000, Dr Loss on sale of receivables 400 / Cr Trade receivables 20,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Cash 18,600 / Cr Liability to factor 18,600</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Customers pay the factor: no entry</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Customers pay the factor 20,000: Dr Liability to factor 18,600, Dr Interest expense 400, Dr Cash 1,000 / Cr Trade receivables 20,000</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Holdback returned: Dr Cash 1,000 / Cr Due from factor 1,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(holdback included above)</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Total cost is 400 either way. The borrowing route keeps 20,000 of receivables and 18,600 of debt in view until the customers pay.</p><p><em>Offsetting</em> is the other way a receivable can shrink without cash. A receivable and a payable with the same party are shown net only when the entity has a legally enforceable right to set them off and intends to settle net or simultaneously (PSAK 232, formerly PSAK 50, IAS 32, para. 42). PHE's arrangement with PT Pertamina (Persero) shows the difference between settling by offset and presenting net. Pertamina funds operations through cash calls that are offset against what it owes for PHE's sales, and Note 44c computes a net payable of 157,404 at the end of 2024 (Daftar 5/184, PDF p. 201). The balance sheet still carries the 18,434 receivable from Pertamina inside related-party trade receivables and the payables separately (Daftar 5/183, PDF p. 200), so PHE has not applied presentation offset.</p><h2>8. Trade receivables across industries</h2><p>The plain model above fits a seller of goods and services on short credit. Four industries change it.</p><p><strong>Banks.</strong> A bank's loans are its trade receivables, but they carry interest and long terms, so the general three-stage approach applies (PSAK 109). For example (illustration), a loan of 10,000 with a 12-month PD of 1 percent and LGD of 40 percent carries 10,000 × 1% × 40% = 40 in stage 1. If a downgrade lifts the lifetime PD to 5 percent, it moves to stage 2 and carries 10,000 × 5% × 40% = 200. The jump from 40 to 200 without any missed payment is the <em>cliff effect</em> of the stage model, and PSAK 107 (formerly PSAK 60, IFRS 7) requires banks to reconcile the allowance by stage.</p><p><strong>Islamic finance.</strong> A murabahah is a sale at cost plus a disclosed margin, paid in instalments. Under PSAK 402 <em>Akuntansi Murabahah</em> (formerly PSAK 102) the seller records the receivable at the amount the customer owes, cost plus margin, with the unearned margin as a contra account. When the seller is a financier that bears no significant inventory risk, as a sharia bank usually is, ISAK 401 (formerly ISAK 101) has it recognise the margin over the term by the effective method. A trader that does carry the inventory risk recognises profit when the goods pass on a short deferred sale. For example (illustration), a financier sells goods costing 100 for 120 over 12 months:</p><blockquote><p>Dr Murabahah receivable 120 / Cr Inventory 100, Cr Deferred murabahah margin 20</p><p>Each month: Dr Cash 10 / Cr Murabahah receivable 10; Dr Deferred margin / Cr Murabahah income for the month's share of the 20 under the effective method</p></blockquote><p>Impairment is where murabahah leaves the plain model. ISAK 402 <em>Penurunan Nilai Piutang Murabahah</em> (formerly ISAK 102) is a bridging interpretation that lets the entity keep its existing impairment policy, incurred loss or regulatory provisioning, until a sharia standard on impairment is issued, so these receivables need not follow PSAK 109's expected credit losses.</p><p><strong>Construction.</strong> Kieso's cost-to-cost bridge example shows why PSAK 115 separates contract assets from receivables. In its first year the builder is 25 percent complete and recognises revenue of 4,500,000 × 25% = 1,125,000, but has billed 900,000 and collected 750,000.</p><blockquote><p>Receivable = billed − collected = 900,000 − 750,000 = 150,000</p><p>Contract asset = revenue − billed = 1,125,000 − 900,000 = 225,000</p></blockquote><p>The receivable is unconditional; the contract asset becomes one only when the next milestone is billed. Kieso's "Billings on construction in process" account does the same job in the older vocabulary.</p><p><strong>Mining and commodity sellers with provisional prices.</strong> Concentrate is often invoiced at a provisional price and settled on a later market average. The receivable then carries price risk on top of credit risk, so its cash flows are not solely principal and interest, and it is measured at fair value through profit or loss under PSAK 109, with price changes kept out of PSAK 115 revenue from customers.</p><p><strong>Lessors</strong> hold lease receivables under PSAK 116 (IFRS 16), where the simplified approach is optional.</p><h2>9. Presentation and analysis: PHE's Note 8</h2><p>PSAK 107 asks for credit-risk information, including a reconciliation of the allowance and the exposure by credit-risk grade, for which a provision matrix by days past due may serve, plus concentrations and collateral. PHE gives receivables by customer, currency and age, and the allowance movement (Note 8, Daftar 5/95–5/96, PDF pp. 112–113).</p><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>31 Dec 2024</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>31 Dec 2023</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Related parties, gross</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2,627,769</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2,129,601</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Less allowance</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(1,586)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(76,531)</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Third parties, gross (local 209,952; foreign 326,556)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>536,508</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>731,142</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Less allowance</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(15,823)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(15,815)</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>Trade receivables, net</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>3,146,868</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>2,768,397</strong></p></td></tr></tbody></table><blockquote><p>Coverage, related parties = 1,586 / 2,627,769 = 0.06% (2023: 3.59%)</p><p>Coverage, third parties = 15,823 / 536,508 = 2.95% (2023: 2.16%)</p></blockquote><p>Three things stand out. First, the related-party allowance fell by 74,945, from 76,531 to 1,586, which is close to the whole fall in the allowance (74,937), so the 2024 release sits almost entirely in that group, although PHE does not split the movement. Note 44c adds that related-party receivables are fully collectible (Daftar 5/184, PDF p. 201). Second, PT Kilang Pertamina Internasional alone owes 2,237,285, 85.1 percent of related-party and 70.7 percent of all gross trade receivables (Note 44c, Daftar 5/183, PDF p. 200), while Note 8 says there is no significant concentration of credit risk. Third, parts of the note keep the pre-PSAK 109 format. The ageing uses the old labels, including "past due and impaired, more than 365 days" for 560,851 against a total allowance of 17,409 (3.1 percent). The risk note says allowances cover "only … losses that have been incurred" (Note 49c(iii), Daftar 5/230, PDF p. 247), and the English text of Note 3g reverses the 12-month ECL condition that the Indonesian states correctly (PDF p. 60). The policy itself treats a receivable as in default at 360 days past due (Note 3g, PDF p. 61), longer than the 90-day presumption of section 5, which the standard allows only with supporting evidence.</p><p>The usual analysis compares revenue with the average balance.</p><blockquote><p>Receivables turnover = 14,330,340 / ((3,146,868 + 2,768,397) / 2) = 4.85 times; days sales outstanding = 365 / 4.85 = 75.3 days</p></blockquote><p>Revenue includes 1,133,213 billed to SKK Migas (Note 35, Daftar 5/148, PDF p. 165), and Note 44c shows no trade receivable from SKK Migas at the end of 2024. Excluding it gives 13,197,127 / 2,957,632.5 = 4.46 times, or 81.8 days. For PHE's largest customer, year-end balance over its 2024 revenue of 7,372,296 (Note 35) gives 2,237,285 / 7,372,296 × 365 = 110.8 days.</p><h2>10. Upstream note: what the receivable does not contain</h2><p>PHE's trade receivables cover only barrels it has lifted and sold. Where a subsidiary lifted less than its entitlement, the shortfall is owed by SKK Migas and sits in due from the Government; PHE's policy says such imbalances "are not transactions with customers" (Note 3p, Daftar 5/63, PDF p. 80). The domestic market obligation fee sits there too. Crude sales to Pertamina moved to PT Kilang Pertamina Internasional from 1 September 2021 (Note 44c).</p><h2>11. Related lines</h2><ul><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/revenues">revenues</a>: the other side of every trade receivable.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/due-from-government">due-from-government</a>: underlifting and DMO fees owed by the state.</p></li><li><p><code>other-receivables-net</code>: nontrade claims under the same policy and allowance method (Note 10).</p></li><li><p><code>long-term-receivables</code>: the non-current claims reported separately.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/production-expenses">other-income-expense-net</a>: where PHE's 57,813 reversal lands (Note 42).</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/finance-cost">finance-income</a>: interest unwound on receivables with a financing component.</p></li></ul><h3>Least-confident assertions</h3><ul><li><p>PSAK 109 paragraph numbers (5.4.4, 5.5.15) and the 30- and 90-day presumptions were confirmed through secondary summaries, not the standard's text. The derecognition tests are stated at the level of principle.</p></li><li><p>The claim that a provisionally priced receivable fails the SPPI test and goes to fair value through profit or loss is from knowledge of IFRS 9 practice in mining and was not checked here.</p></li><li><p>Murabahah points (receivable at cost plus margin, ISAK 401's effective method for financiers, ISAK 402 keeping existing impairment policies) rest on secondary sources; the adopted texts were not opened. Keeping the receivable at the invoice with a refund liability for an expected prompt-payment discount is one treatment; measuring it at the transaction price under PSAK 109.5.1.3 is another.</p></li><li><p>Placing the 2024 release in the related-party group is inferred from the change in the allowance by customer group; PHE does not split the movement. Reading the ageing labels as a carried-over IAS 39 format is this page's interpretation.</p></li><li><p>That PHE's 360-day default definition departs from the 90-day presumption is this page's reading; PHE may hold evidence that rebuts it, which it does not disclose.</p></li></ul><h2>References</h2><ol><li><p>PT Pertamina Hulu Energi dan Entitas Anak, consolidated financial statements 31 December 2024 (audited, USD thousand): statement of financial position (Daftar 1/1, PDF p. 11); Note 3g (Daftar 5/42–5/44, PDF pp. 59–61); Note 3h (Daftar 5/51, PDF p. 68); Note 3p (Daftar 5/62–5/63, PDF pp. 79–80); Note 4b(vi) (Daftar 5/84, PDF p. 101); Note 8 (Daftar 5/95–5/96, PDF pp. 112–113); Note 35 (Daftar 5/148, PDF p. 165); Note 42 (Daftar 5/150, PDF p. 167); Note 44c (Daftar 5/183–5/184, PDF pp. 200–201); Note 49c (Daftar 5/229–5/230, PDF pp. 246–247). VERIFIED (PHE FS 2024, on disk).</p></li><li><p>Lecture notes this series follows, "Inter 2 – Chapter 18 – Revenue Recognition" (Rumpun Akuntansi Keuangan): five steps, variable consideration (expected value vs most likely amount), time value of money (the notes' Pertamina–Shell adaptation of Kieso's zero-interest note), sales returns, cost-to-cost bridge example. NOTES. The 2019 interest accrual is dated 1 December in the notes; corrected here to 31 December.</p></li><li><p>Lecture notes, "Inter 2 – Chapter 17 – Investments": impairment section. NOTES. Its incurred-loss approach is outdated and is corrected here to PSAK 109 ECL.</p></li><li><p>The lecture notes have no chapter on cash and receivables (Inter 1 Ch 7 is missing), so sections 1, 3, 6 and 7 follow Kieso's chapter order (ref. 5).</p></li><li><p>Kieso, Weygandt and Warfield, <em>Intermediate Accounting: IFRS Edition</em> (Wiley), chapters "Cash and Receivables" (classification, trade and cash discounts, sales returns, allowance and direct write-off methods, assignment and factoring) and "Revenue Recognition". Sections 1, 3, 6 and 7 follow the receivables chapter order from knowledge of the textbook; edition and page numbers not checked. STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.</p></li><li><p>PSAK 115 (IFRS 15), para. 108 (receivable as an unconditional right; difference on initial recognition presented as an expense) and para. 107 (contract assets). VERIFIED (web, AASB 15 text, standards.aasb.gov.au). Para. 63 (one-year practical expedient) VERIFIED (web, NSW Treasury TC19-07 quoting AASB 15.63). Paras. 47 and 60–65 otherwise STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.</p></li><li><p>PSAK 109 (IFRS 9): simplified approach required for trade receivables and contract assets without a significant financing component (5.5.15) VERIFIED (web, IASB AP27C May 2024 and BDO summaries); 30-day and 90-day rebuttable presumptions VERIFIED (web, IASB 2013 staff paper AP5B and RBI Ind AS 109 draft); derecognition and factoring outcomes VERIFIED (web, KPMG India and IFRS Community factoring guidance, secondary). Para. 5.4.4 and classification tests STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.</p></li><li><p>PSAK 232 (IAS 32) para. 42 offsetting; PSAK 201 (IAS 1); PSAK 107 (IFRS 7), with para. 35N (provision matrix as risk profile) VERIFIED (web, IFRS Foundation taxonomy illustrative example 19); PSAK 116 (IFRS 16). Numbers and titles VERIFIED (web, IAI, <em>Perubahan Penomoran PSAK dan ISAK dalam SAK Indonesia</em>); other content STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.</p></li><li><p>PSAK 402 <em>Akuntansi Murabahah</em>, ISAK 401 and ISAK 402. Receivable at cost plus margin and profit timing VERIFIED (web, secondary: journal summaries of PSAK 102 paras. 22–24); ISAK 401 effective method for sellers without significant inventory risk and ISAK 402 as a bridging impairment interpretation VERIFIED (web, secondary: BINUS Accounting, "Pengesahan PSAK 102 (2019), ISAK 101 dan ISAK 102", 13 Dec 2019). Adopted texts not opened.</p></li></ol>$c$,
       content_json = $j${"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"In brief","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A trade receivable is born with revenue, at the moment control of goods or services passes to a customer and the seller's right to payment depends only on time. Revenue is measured at the PSAK 115 transaction price, net of trade discounts and of the discounts, returns and financing the seller expects, and the receivable records the unconditional right to payment.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The \"net\" in the line is an allowance for expected credit losses (ECL) under PSAK 109. Trade receivables without a significant financing component must use the simplified approach, lifetime losses from day one, usually through a provision matrix of loss rates by days past due adjusted for forecasts. The old incurred-loss model, which waited for a loss event, is gone.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A receivable leaves the balance sheet when it is collected, written off because recovery is no longer reasonably expected, or transferred with substantially all its risks and rewards. Factoring without recourse is a sale; factoring with recourse usually stays on the balance sheet as a secured borrowing.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"horizontalRule"},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PT Pertamina Hulu Energi and subsidiaries (PHE) report trade receivables, net, of 3,146,868 at 31 December 2024 against 2,768,397 a year earlier, in USD thousand (PHE FS 2024, statement of financial position, Daftar 1/1, PDF p. 11; \"Daftar\" is the printed page). The line is 10.3 percent of total assets and 39.2 percent of current assets (9.0 and 28.0 percent a year earlier), the largest current asset PHE has. This page uses those numbers as the starting point for its examples.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"After reading it a student should be able to say when a receivable arises, measure it at recognition and at the reporting date, record write-offs, recoveries and transfers, and see where banks, Islamic financiers, builders and miners depart from the plain model.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"All amounts are in USD thousand unless stated. Figures from PHE's statements are cited to their note. Every other number is labelled an illustration.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1. What a trade receivable is","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A receivable is a claim on someone else for cash. Kieso sorts claims three ways. ","type":"text"},{"text":"Trade receivables","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" arise from selling goods or services in the ordinary course of business; ","type":"text"},{"text":"nontrade receivables","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" arise from anything else, such as insurance claims, advances to employees or deposits. ","type":"text"},{"text":"Accounts receivable","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" are open-account promises with no formal instrument; ","type":"text"},{"text":"notes receivable","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" are written promises to pay a sum at a date, usually with interest. And receivables are ","type":"text"},{"text":"current","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" if collection is expected within the operating cycle or twelve months, ","type":"text"},{"text":"non-current","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" otherwise (PSAK 201, formerly PSAK 1, IAS 1).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PHE's policy uses the first split. Trade receivables are amounts due from customers for crude oil, natural gas, drilling services, oil and gas support services, and energy distribution and logistics services; other receivables are amounts from third or related parties \"beyond the ordinary course of business\" (Note 3h, Daftar 5/51, PDF p. 68). Collection within one period puts a receivable in current assets, otherwise non-current (same note). PHE's separate \"long term receivables\" of 2,087 (2023: 2,997) carry no note reference on the face.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 115 (formerly PSAK 72, IFRS 15) adds a distinction that matters most for builders. A ","type":"text"},{"text":"receivable","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" is a right to consideration that is unconditional, meaning only the passage of time is required before payment falls due (para. 108). A ","type":"text"},{"text":"contract asset","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" is a right that still depends on something other than time, typically further performance.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2. Recognition: the receivable follows revenue","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A receivable appears in the same journal entry as revenue, so \"when do we record it?\" is the revenue question. Kieso's ","type":"text"},{"text":"Intermediate Accounting","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" sets out the five steps of PSAK 115. Identify the contract, identify the performance obligations, determine the transaction price, allocate it, and recognise revenue when each obligation is satisfied, that is, when the customer obtains control.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"For a crude seller, control typically passes at lifting, on FOB terms when the cargo crosses the custody point into the buyer's tanker. PHE recognises crude and gas revenue \"based on the actual lifting amount and sold to customers\" (Note 3p, Daftar 5/62, PDF p. 79). For example (illustration), a PHE subsidiary lifts 600 thousand barrels for a domestic refinery at USD 75 per barrel, payable in 30 days.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Invoice = 600 × 75 = 45,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Trade receivables 45,000 / Cr Revenue from crude oil 45,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"There is no matching \"Dr Cost of goods sold / Cr Inventory\" of the kind a merchandiser records. Note 11 lists only materials, and Note 3p charges the related costs on the amount actually sold, so the cost of producing the barrels has already gone to expense.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Signing a contract creates no receivable. Until delivery each side holds only an unperformed promise, and the two cancel out.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"3. Measurement at recognition","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The receivable starts at the transaction price, the consideration the seller expects to be entitled to (PSAK 115.47). Four things can make that differ from the list price.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Trade discounts","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" are price reductions given to a class of customer. They never reach the books, so a list price of 80 per barrel less a 6.25 percent volume discount is recorded at 75.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cash discounts","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" reward prompt payment. Kieso gives two methods, mirror images of the purchase methods in the inventories essay. The ","type":"text"},{"text":"gross method","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" records the full invoice and the discount only when taken; the ","type":"text"},{"text":"net method","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" records the discounted amount and shows a discount the customer forfeits as income. For example (illustration), a PHE drilling subsidiary bills 2,000 on terms of 2/10, net 30, so prompt payment saves the customer 2,000 × 2% = 40.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Gross method","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Net method","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Invoice","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Trade receivables 2,000 / Cr Service revenue 2,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Trade receivables 1,960 / Cr Service revenue 1,960","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Paid within 10 days","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Cash 1,960, Dr Sales discounts 40 / Cr Trade receivables 2,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Cash 1,960 / Cr Trade receivables 1,960","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Paid late","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Cash 2,000 / Cr Trade receivables 2,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Cash 2,000 / Cr Trade receivables 1,960, Cr Sales discounts forfeited 40","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 115 replaces this choice with an estimate. A prompt-payment discount is ","type":"text"},{"text":"variable consideration","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", so the seller includes in revenue the amount it expects, using the expected value or the most likely amount. If 70 percent of such customers take the discount:","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Expected transaction price = 2,000 − 70% × 40 = 1,972","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A common treatment keeps the receivable at the invoice amount of 2,000, which is what the customer owes if it pays late, and holds the expected discount of 28 as a refund liability.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Sales returns and allowances","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" follow the same logic. A crude cargo rarely comes back, but price allowances for off-specification quality do happen. If a buyer is expected to claim 1 percent on the 45,000 cargo in section 2, revenue is 44,550 and the 450 sits as a refund liability (or as a credit to the receivable once agreed).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Revenue from crude oil (allowance) 450 / Cr Refund liability 450","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Time value.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" When payment comes long after delivery, part of what the customer pays is interest, and PSAK 115 takes it out of revenue (paras. 60–65). An entity may ignore it when it expects at contract inception that the gap will be one year or less (para. 63), which is why a 30-day crude invoice is never discounted. The lecture notes this series follows adapt Kieso's long case to a crude cargo sold on 1 July 2019 for a four-year zero-interest note when the market rate is 12 percent. Revenue is the cash price of 1,000,000, the note's face value is 1,000,000 × 1.57352 (the four-year factor at 12 percent) = 1,573,520, and interest is 1,000,000 × 12% × 6/12 = 60,000 for 2019 (accrued at 31 December) and 1,060,000 × 12% = 127,200 for 2020.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"For example (illustration), a PHE subsidiary bills 10,500 for a drilling campaign and agrees that the customer pays three annual instalments of 3,500 starting a year after completion on 31 December 2024, with no interest. The customer would pay 8 percent a year to borrow.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PV of 2025 instalment = 3,500 / 1.08 = 3,240.74","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PV of 2026 instalment = 3,500 / 1.08² = 3,000.69","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PV of 2027 instalment = 3,500 / 1.08³ = 2,778.41","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Revenue and receivable at 31 Dec 2024 = 9,019.84; interest to be earned = 10,500 − 9,019.84 = 1,480.16","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Year","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Opening","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Interest income at 8%","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Instalment","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Closing","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2025","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"9,019.84","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"721.59","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(3,500.00)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"6,241.43","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2026","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"6,241.43","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"499.31","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(3,500.00)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"3,240.74","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2027","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"3,240.74","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"259.26","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(3,500.00)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"0.00","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"31 Dec 2024: Dr Trade receivables 9,019.84 / Cr Service revenue 9,019.84","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"31 Dec 2025: Dr Trade receivables 721.59 / Cr Interest income 721.59; Dr Cash 3,500 / Cr Trade receivables 3,500","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The current/non-current boundary runs through this receivable. One method puts the present value of the 2025 instalment, 3,240.74, in current assets and 5,779.10 in non-current. The other puts in current assets only the fall in carrying amount during 2025, 9,019.84 − 6,241.43 = 2,778.41, leaving 6,241.43 non-current. PSAK 201 prescribes neither; the choice must be applied consistently.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"4. Subsequent measurement: amortised cost","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 109 (formerly PSAK 71, IFRS 9) classifies a financial asset by two tests: the business model (is it held to collect contractual cash flows?) and the cash flows (are they solely payments of principal and interest, SPPI?). A trade receivable passes both, so it is measured at ","type":"text"},{"text":"amortised cost","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":": the initial amount, plus interest at the effective interest rate (EIR), less cash collected, less the loss allowance. For a 30-day invoice the EIR is effectively nil, so amortised cost is the invoice less cash and allowance. For the instalment receivable above it is the schedule.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PHE's policy says trade and other receivables are \"recognized initially at fair value and subsequently measured at amortized cost using the effective interest method, less provision for impairment\" (Note 3h). PSAK 109 actually starts trade receivables without a significant financing component at the transaction price, which for short invoices is the same number.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"5. Expected credit losses","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Why the model changed.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Under IAS 39 an asset was written down only when there was objective evidence that it was impaired. That was the ","type":"text"},{"text":"incurred-loss","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" model. It waited for a loss event, a missed payment or a bankruptcy, so allowances arrived late, typically after the recession that caused them. PSAK 109 replaced it with ","type":"text"},{"text":"expected credit losses","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", the probability-weighted present value of cash shortfalls, recognised before any default. Kieso's ageing schedule survives as the provision matrix.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Two approaches.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" The ","type":"text"},{"text":"general approach","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" has three stages. A new asset carries 12-month ECL (stage 1). After a significant increase in credit risk, presumed when payments are more than 30 days past due, it carries lifetime ECL (stage 2). When it is credit-impaired it carries lifetime ECL and earns interest only on the net amount (stage 3); default is presumed no later than 90 days past due. Both presumptions are rebuttable with reasonable and supportable evidence. The ","type":"text"},{"text":"simplified approach","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" skips the tracking and recognises lifetime ECL from day one. It is required for trade receivables and contract assets without a significant financing component and is a policy choice for those with one, and for lease receivables (PSAK 109.5.5.15).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PHE applies the simplified approach because its receivables \"do not contain significant financing component\" (Note 3g, Daftar 5/43, PDF p. 60).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The provision matrix.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" A matrix groups receivables that share credit risk, ages each group by days past due, and applies a loss rate to each bucket, drawn from history and adjusted for forecasts (PHE Note 4b(vi), Daftar 5/84, PDF p. 101). For example (illustration), PHE's third-party gross receivables of 536,508 at the end of 2024 (Note 8a) are split into five buckets. The split is assumed; PHE ages only the total.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Days past due","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Gross","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Historical rate","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Adjusted rate (× 1.10)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Allowance","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Not past due","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"380,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"0.30%","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"0.33%","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1,254.00","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1–90","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"120,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1.50%","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1.65%","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1,980.00","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"91–180","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"6,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"10.00%","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"11.00%","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"660.00","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"181–365","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"18,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"25.00%","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"27.50%","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"4,950.00","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Over 365","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"12,508","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"60.00%","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"66.00%","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"8,255.28","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Total","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"536,508","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"17,099.28","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"At historical rates = 1,140.00 + 1,800.00 + 600.00 + 4,500.00 + 7,504.80 = 15,544.80","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Forward-looking adjustment = 17,099.28 − 15,544.80 = 1,554.48","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The adjustment needs a reason that links a forecast to customers' ability to pay. PHE names gross domestic product as the most relevant factor and adjusts historical loss rates for its expected change (Note 49c, Daftar 5/229, PDF p. 246). Here the illustration assumes that a forecast one-point fall in GDP growth has, in past cycles, raised loss rates by a tenth. Current receivables carry an allowance too, which the incurred-loss model rarely gave them.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Grouping matters.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" A matrix is valid only for receivables that share credit characteristics. PHE's related-party receivables of 2,627,769 are owed mostly by state-owned companies. Applying the third-party average rate (17,099.28 / 536,508 = 3.19 percent) to them would give an allowance of about 83,751. A rate built from the debtor's own probability of default (PD) and loss given default (LGD), which PHE says its models use (Note 49c), gives a much smaller number. Assume a one-year PD of 0.3 percent, LGD of 40 percent and an average time to collection of 60 days:","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2,627,769 × 0.3% × 40% × 60 / 365 = 518.35","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Recording.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" The allowance is a contra account, so the ledger keeps each customer at the gross amount. Starting from PHE's opening third-party allowance of 15,815 (Note 8a, 2023):","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Charge for the year = 17,099.28 − 15,815.00 = 1,284.28","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Impairment loss on trade receivables 1,284.28 / Cr Allowance for expected credit losses 1,284.28","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"6. Write-offs and recoveries","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A write-off removes the part of a receivable the entity has no reasonable expectation of recovering (PSAK 109.5.4.4; PHE Note 3g, Daftar 5/44, PDF p. 61). Under the allowance method it does not touch profit, because the loss was expensed when the allowance was built. For example (illustration), a third-party gas customer owing 1,200 is declared bankrupt with nothing for unsecured creditors.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Event","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Allowance method","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Direct write-off method","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Write-off","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Allowance 1,200 / Cr Trade receivables 1,200","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Bad debt expense 1,200 / Cr Trade receivables 1,200","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"300 recovered later","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Trade receivables 300 / Cr Allowance 300; Dr Cash 300 / Cr Trade receivables 300","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Cash 300 / Cr Bad debt recovery 300","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Kieso keeps the direct method for illustration only. It puts the expense in the year a customer fails instead of the year of the sale, and it carries the receivable at more than will be collected. PSAK 109 does not permit it.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The year's expense is whatever brings the allowance to its required balance after write-offs and recoveries.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Impairment expense = closing allowance − opening allowance + write-offs − recoveries","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PHE's 2024 movement has no write-offs. It runs from 92,346 at the start of the year, less a reversal of 57,813 and a foreign exchange deduction of 17,124, to 17,409 (Note 8d, Daftar 5/96, PDF p. 113). The reversal appears as \"Impairment and correction of receivables\" of 57,813 in other income (Note 42, Daftar 5/150, PDF p. 167), so 2024 profit gained that amount from releasing allowances.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"7. Derecognition, factoring and offsetting","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A receivable can be turned into cash before it is due. Kieso describes two routes. ","type":"text"},{"text":"Assignment or pledging","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" uses receivables as security for a loan; the receivables stay on the books and the loan is a liability. ","type":"text"},{"text":"Factoring","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" sells them to a finance company, the factor, which then collects from the customers.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Whether a factoring is a sale turns on PSAK 109's derecognition tests. An asset is derecognised when the rights to its cash flows expire, or when they are transferred together with substantially all the risks and rewards of ownership. If the seller keeps substantially all the risks, typically by guaranteeing the factor against bad debts (","type":"text"},{"text":"recourse","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":"), the asset stays on the balance sheet and the cash is a borrowing. If risks are neither substantially transferred nor kept, the asset goes if the buyer controls it and stays to the extent of continuing involvement if not (PHE Note 3g, Daftar 5/42, PDF p. 59, summarises the same tests).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"For example (illustration), a subsidiary factors 20,000 of gas receivables. The factor charges 2 percent and holds back 5 percent, 1,000, against disputes, so it pays 18,600 now.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Without recourse (sale)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"With recourse (secured borrowing)","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Cash 18,600, Dr Due from factor 1,000, Dr Loss on sale of receivables 400 / Cr Trade receivables 20,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Cash 18,600 / Cr Liability to factor 18,600","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Customers pay the factor: no entry","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Customers pay the factor 20,000: Dr Liability to factor 18,600, Dr Interest expense 400, Dr Cash 1,000 / Cr Trade receivables 20,000","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Holdback returned: Dr Cash 1,000 / Cr Due from factor 1,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(holdback included above)","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Total cost is 400 either way. The borrowing route keeps 20,000 of receivables and 18,600 of debt in view until the customers pay.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Offsetting","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" is the other way a receivable can shrink without cash. A receivable and a payable with the same party are shown net only when the entity has a legally enforceable right to set them off and intends to settle net or simultaneously (PSAK 232, formerly PSAK 50, IAS 32, para. 42). PHE's arrangement with PT Pertamina (Persero) shows the difference between settling by offset and presenting net. Pertamina funds operations through cash calls that are offset against what it owes for PHE's sales, and Note 44c computes a net payable of 157,404 at the end of 2024 (Daftar 5/184, PDF p. 201). The balance sheet still carries the 18,434 receivable from Pertamina inside related-party trade receivables and the payables separately (Daftar 5/183, PDF p. 200), so PHE has not applied presentation offset.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"8. Trade receivables across industries","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The plain model above fits a seller of goods and services on short credit. Four industries change it.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Banks.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" A bank's loans are its trade receivables, but they carry interest and long terms, so the general three-stage approach applies (PSAK 109). For example (illustration), a loan of 10,000 with a 12-month PD of 1 percent and LGD of 40 percent carries 10,000 × 1% × 40% = 40 in stage 1. If a downgrade lifts the lifetime PD to 5 percent, it moves to stage 2 and carries 10,000 × 5% × 40% = 200. The jump from 40 to 200 without any missed payment is the ","type":"text"},{"text":"cliff effect","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" of the stage model, and PSAK 107 (formerly PSAK 60, IFRS 7) requires banks to reconcile the allowance by stage.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Islamic finance.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" A murabahah is a sale at cost plus a disclosed margin, paid in instalments. Under PSAK 402 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Akuntansi Murabahah","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (formerly PSAK 102) the seller records the receivable at the amount the customer owes, cost plus margin, with the unearned margin as a contra account. When the seller is a financier that bears no significant inventory risk, as a sharia bank usually is, ISAK 401 (formerly ISAK 101) has it recognise the margin over the term by the effective method. A trader that does carry the inventory risk recognises profit when the goods pass on a short deferred sale. For example (illustration), a financier sells goods costing 100 for 120 over 12 months:","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Murabahah receivable 120 / Cr Inventory 100, Cr Deferred murabahah margin 20","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Each month: Dr Cash 10 / Cr Murabahah receivable 10; Dr Deferred margin / Cr Murabahah income for the month's share of the 20 under the effective method","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Impairment is where murabahah leaves the plain model. ISAK 402 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Penurunan Nilai Piutang Murabahah","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (formerly ISAK 102) is a bridging interpretation that lets the entity keep its existing impairment policy, incurred loss or regulatory provisioning, until a sharia standard on impairment is issued, so these receivables need not follow PSAK 109's expected credit losses.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Construction.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Kieso's cost-to-cost bridge example shows why PSAK 115 separates contract assets from receivables. In its first year the builder is 25 percent complete and recognises revenue of 4,500,000 × 25% = 1,125,000, but has billed 900,000 and collected 750,000.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Receivable = billed − collected = 900,000 − 750,000 = 150,000","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Contract asset = revenue − billed = 1,125,000 − 900,000 = 225,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The receivable is unconditional; the contract asset becomes one only when the next milestone is billed. Kieso's \"Billings on construction in process\" account does the same job in the older vocabulary.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Mining and commodity sellers with provisional prices.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Concentrate is often invoiced at a provisional price and settled on a later market average. The receivable then carries price risk on top of credit risk, so its cash flows are not solely principal and interest, and it is measured at fair value through profit or loss under PSAK 109, with price changes kept out of PSAK 115 revenue from customers.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Lessors","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" hold lease receivables under PSAK 116 (IFRS 16), where the simplified approach is optional.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"9. Presentation and analysis: PHE's Note 8","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 107 asks for credit-risk information, including a reconciliation of the allowance and the exposure by credit-risk grade, for which a provision matrix by days past due may serve, plus concentrations and collateral. PHE gives receivables by customer, currency and age, and the allowance movement (Note 8, Daftar 5/95–5/96, PDF pp. 112–113).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"31 Dec 2024","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"31 Dec 2023","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Related parties, gross","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2,627,769","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2,129,601","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Less allowance","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(1,586)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(76,531)","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Third parties, gross (local 209,952; foreign 326,556)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"536,508","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"731,142","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Less allowance","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(15,823)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(15,815)","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Trade receivables, net","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"3,146,868","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2,768,397","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Coverage, related parties = 1,586 / 2,627,769 = 0.06% (2023: 3.59%)","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Coverage, third parties = 15,823 / 536,508 = 2.95% (2023: 2.16%)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Three things stand out. First, the related-party allowance fell by 74,945, from 76,531 to 1,586, which is close to the whole fall in the allowance (74,937), so the 2024 release sits almost entirely in that group, although PHE does not split the movement. Note 44c adds that related-party receivables are fully collectible (Daftar 5/184, PDF p. 201). Second, PT Kilang Pertamina Internasional alone owes 2,237,285, 85.1 percent of related-party and 70.7 percent of all gross trade receivables (Note 44c, Daftar 5/183, PDF p. 200), while Note 8 says there is no significant concentration of credit risk. Third, parts of the note keep the pre-PSAK 109 format. The ageing uses the old labels, including \"past due and impaired, more than 365 days\" for 560,851 against a total allowance of 17,409 (3.1 percent). The risk note says allowances cover \"only … losses that have been incurred\" (Note 49c(iii), Daftar 5/230, PDF p. 247), and the English text of Note 3g reverses the 12-month ECL condition that the Indonesian states correctly (PDF p. 60). The policy itself treats a receivable as in default at 360 days past due (Note 3g, PDF p. 61), longer than the 90-day presumption of section 5, which the standard allows only with supporting evidence.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The usual analysis compares revenue with the average balance.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Receivables turnover = 14,330,340 / ((3,146,868 + 2,768,397) / 2) = 4.85 times; days sales outstanding = 365 / 4.85 = 75.3 days","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Revenue includes 1,133,213 billed to SKK Migas (Note 35, Daftar 5/148, PDF p. 165), and Note 44c shows no trade receivable from SKK Migas at the end of 2024. Excluding it gives 13,197,127 / 2,957,632.5 = 4.46 times, or 81.8 days. For PHE's largest customer, year-end balance over its 2024 revenue of 7,372,296 (Note 35) gives 2,237,285 / 7,372,296 × 365 = 110.8 days.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"10. Upstream note: what the receivable does not contain","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PHE's trade receivables cover only barrels it has lifted and sold. Where a subsidiary lifted less than its entitlement, the shortfall is owed by SKK Migas and sits in due from the Government; PHE's policy says such imbalances \"are not transactions with customers\" (Note 3p, Daftar 5/63, PDF p. 80). The domestic market obligation fee sits there too. Crude sales to Pertamina moved to PT Kilang Pertamina Internasional from 1 September 2021 (Note 44c).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"11. Related lines","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"revenues","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/revenues","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": the other side of every trade receivable.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"due-from-government","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/due-from-government","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": underlifting and DMO fees owed by the state.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"other-receivables-net","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"text":": nontrade claims under the same policy and allowance method (Note 10).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"long-term-receivables","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"text":": the non-current claims reported separately.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"other-income-expense-net","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/production-expenses","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": where PHE's 57,813 reversal lands (Note 42).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"finance-income","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/finance-cost","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": interest unwound on receivables with a financing component.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Least-confident assertions","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 109 paragraph numbers (5.4.4, 5.5.15) and the 30- and 90-day presumptions were confirmed through secondary summaries, not the standard's text. The derecognition tests are stated at the level of principle.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The claim that a provisionally priced receivable fails the SPPI test and goes to fair value through profit or loss is from knowledge of IFRS 9 practice in mining and was not checked here.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Murabahah points (receivable at cost plus margin, ISAK 401's effective method for financiers, ISAK 402 keeping existing impairment policies) rest on secondary sources; the adopted texts were not opened. Keeping the receivable at the invoice with a refund liability for an expected prompt-payment discount is one treatment; measuring it at the transaction price under PSAK 109.5.1.3 is another.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Placing the 2024 release in the related-party group is inferred from the change in the allowance by customer group; PHE does not split the movement. Reading the ageing labels as a carried-over IAS 39 format is this page's interpretation.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"That PHE's 360-day default definition departs from the 90-day presumption is this page's reading; PHE may hold evidence that rebuts it, which it does not disclose.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"References","type":"text"}]},{"type":"orderedList","attrs":{"type":null,"start":1},"content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PT Pertamina Hulu Energi dan Entitas Anak, consolidated financial statements 31 December 2024 (audited, USD thousand): statement of financial position (Daftar 1/1, PDF p. 11); Note 3g (Daftar 5/42–5/44, PDF pp. 59–61); Note 3h (Daftar 5/51, PDF p. 68); Note 3p (Daftar 5/62–5/63, PDF pp. 79–80); Note 4b(vi) (Daftar 5/84, PDF p. 101); Note 8 (Daftar 5/95–5/96, PDF pp. 112–113); Note 35 (Daftar 5/148, PDF p. 165); Note 42 (Daftar 5/150, PDF p. 167); Note 44c (Daftar 5/183–5/184, PDF pp. 200–201); Note 49c (Daftar 5/229–5/230, PDF pp. 246–247). VERIFIED (PHE FS 2024, on disk).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Lecture notes this series follows, \"Inter 2 – Chapter 18 – Revenue Recognition\" (Rumpun Akuntansi Keuangan): five steps, variable consideration (expected value vs most likely amount), time value of money (the notes' Pertamina–Shell adaptation of Kieso's zero-interest note), sales returns, cost-to-cost bridge example. NOTES. The 2019 interest accrual is dated 1 December in the notes; corrected here to 31 December.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Lecture notes, \"Inter 2 – Chapter 17 – Investments\": impairment section. NOTES. Its incurred-loss approach is outdated and is corrected here to PSAK 109 ECL.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The lecture notes have no chapter on cash and receivables (Inter 1 Ch 7 is missing), so sections 1, 3, 6 and 7 follow Kieso's chapter order (ref. 5).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Kieso, Weygandt and Warfield, ","type":"text"},{"text":"Intermediate Accounting: IFRS Edition","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (Wiley), chapters \"Cash and Receivables\" (classification, trade and cash discounts, sales returns, allowance and direct write-off methods, assignment and factoring) and \"Revenue Recognition\". Sections 1, 3, 6 and 7 follow the receivables chapter order from knowledge of the textbook; edition and page numbers not checked. STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 115 (IFRS 15), para. 108 (receivable as an unconditional right; difference on initial recognition presented as an expense) and para. 107 (contract assets). VERIFIED (web, AASB 15 text, standards.aasb.gov.au). Para. 63 (one-year practical expedient) VERIFIED (web, NSW Treasury TC19-07 quoting AASB 15.63). Paras. 47 and 60–65 otherwise STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 109 (IFRS 9): simplified approach required for trade receivables and contract assets without a significant financing component (5.5.15) VERIFIED (web, IASB AP27C May 2024 and BDO summaries); 30-day and 90-day rebuttable presumptions VERIFIED (web, IASB 2013 staff paper AP5B and RBI Ind AS 109 draft); derecognition and factoring outcomes VERIFIED (web, KPMG India and IFRS Community factoring guidance, secondary). Para. 5.4.4 and classification tests STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 232 (IAS 32) para. 42 offsetting; PSAK 201 (IAS 1); PSAK 107 (IFRS 7), with para. 35N (provision matrix as risk profile) VERIFIED (web, IFRS Foundation taxonomy illustrative example 19); PSAK 116 (IFRS 16). Numbers and titles VERIFIED (web, IAI, ","type":"text"},{"text":"Perubahan Penomoran PSAK dan ISAK dalam SAK Indonesia","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":"); other content STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 402 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Akuntansi Murabahah","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", ISAK 401 and ISAK 402. Receivable at cost plus margin and profit timing VERIFIED (web, secondary: journal summaries of PSAK 102 paras. 22–24); ISAK 401 effective method for sellers without significant inventory risk and ISAK 402 as a bridging impairment interpretation VERIFIED (web, secondary: BINUS Accounting, \"Pengesahan PSAK 102 (2019), ISAK 101 dan ISAK 102\", 13 Dec 2019). Adopted texts not opened.","type":"text"}]}]}]}]}$j$::jsonb,
       presentation = $p${"deck":"The theory of trade receivables from the sale that creates them to the factor that buys them, worked through PHE's crude and gas customers and set beside the industries with their own rules","genre":null,"author_bio":null,"references":[],"hero_caption":null,"key_takeaways":["A trade receivable is born with revenue, at the moment control of goods or services passes to a customer and the seller's right to payment depends only on time. Revenue is measured at the PSAK 115 transaction price, net of trade discounts and of the discounts, returns and financing the seller expects, and the receivable records the unconditional right to payment.","The \"net\" in the line is an allowance for expected credit losses (ECL) under PSAK 109. Trade receivables without a significant financing component must use the simplified approach, lifetime losses from day one, usually through a provision matrix of loss rates by days past due adjusted for forecasts. The old incurred-loss model, which waited for a loss event, is gone.","A receivable leaves the balance sheet when it is collected, written off because recovery is no longer reasonably expected, or transferred with substantially all its risks and rewards. Factoring without recourse is a sale; factoring with recourse usually stays on the balance sheet as a secured borrowing."],"revision_note":null}$p$::jsonb,
       updated_at = now()
 WHERE id = 'ff2f67b2-8acc-408d-84c4-c5d044c7d648';
INSERT INTO public.essay_revisions (essay_id, revision_no, change_type, title, snippet, content_json, status, change_summary, changed_by)
SELECT e.id, coalesce((SELECT max(x.revision_no) FROM public.essay_revisions x WHERE x.essay_id = e.id), 0) + 1, 'rollback',
       e.title, e.snippet, e.content_json, e.status, $s$Restored from the snapshot taken before the lecture-notes removal$s$, null
  FROM public.essays e WHERE e.id = 'ff2f67b2-8acc-408d-84c4-c5d044c7d648';

DO $chk$ BEGIN
  IF (SELECT count(*) FROM public.essays WHERE id IN ('a1561fc6-765d-4fc0-84b1-a5da1a890033', 'ff2f67b2-8acc-408d-84c4-c5d044c7d648') AND updated_at = now()) <> 2 THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION 'Not written: an essay in this part was changed after the snapshot. Nothing in this part was saved.';
  END IF;
END $chk$;
COMMIT;
